1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Life without cars is almost impossible to imagine.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
But how do they really work?

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
And who developed all the modern features we've come to rely on?

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Well, roll up your sleeves for a real nuts and bolts tour under the hood of the modern car.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, The Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Hi, I'm Ron Hazleton, and this is The Body Shop on the History Channel.

7
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:54,160
There's nothing I like more than taking things apart and putting them back together.

8
00:00:54,161 --> 00:00:59,480
And in the next two hours, with the help of my two highly trained, certified technicians,

9
00:00:59,481 --> 00:01:05,540
Bob and Chris, we're going to take one of America's most popular cars, this Ford Taurus,

10
00:01:05,541 --> 00:01:08,340
and sacrifice it on the altar of knowledge.

11
00:01:08,341 --> 00:01:11,980
We're going to unscrew it, unbolt it, and generally rip it apart.

12
00:01:11,981 --> 00:01:17,560
Now, there's no way we'll have time to go into all of the 14,000 parts, but by the end

13
00:01:17,561 --> 00:01:21,380
of the program, you'll really know what makes a car tick.

14
00:01:21,381 --> 00:01:24,940
You'll learn the stories behind all of today's automotive systems.

15
00:01:24,941 --> 00:01:29,220
So put on your metal overalls and let's go to work.

16
00:01:29,221 --> 00:01:33,260
Now it's only fitting that we start with the heart of any car, the engine.

17
00:01:33,261 --> 00:01:35,940
Bob, Chris, let's take her out.

18
00:01:35,941 --> 00:01:36,940
Okay, Ron.

19
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:51,340
Most people don't think about the controlled chaos that takes place in an engine

20
00:01:51,341 --> 00:01:54,100
once we turn the key and step on the gas.

21
00:01:54,101 --> 00:01:57,640
The really exciting action takes place inside the cylinders,

22
00:01:57,641 --> 00:02:00,440
which are located under here, the cylinder heads.

23
00:02:00,441 --> 00:02:02,380
Bob, Chris, let's open her up.

24
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:12,400
Imagine, while you're driving,

25
00:02:12,401 --> 00:02:14,800
thousands of miniature explosions per minute

26
00:02:14,801 --> 00:02:17,100
are blasting away in the cylinders.

27
00:02:17,101 --> 00:02:20,240
That's why it's called an internal combustion engine.

28
00:02:20,241 --> 00:02:21,460
Now, here's how it works.

29
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:26,000
When the engine's running, these little valves open up,

30
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:29,540
allowing a fine mist of gasoline and air to enter the cylinder.

31
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:32,880
The explosions occur at the tip of the spark plug.

32
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:34,560
You can see the end of one right here.

33
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:38,700
Now that forces the pistons down into the cylinder,

34
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:41,500
causing the crankshaft back here to spin.

35
00:02:41,501 --> 00:02:45,060
The faster the crankshaft spins, the faster you go.

36
00:02:45,061 --> 00:02:52,060
Today's engines propel cars with amazing power and efficiency.

37
00:02:52,061 --> 00:03:00,060
And combining cars with computers has transformed virtually every system, especially engines,

38
00:03:00,061 --> 00:03:05,060
also known in body shop parlance as power plants.

39
00:03:05,061 --> 00:03:08,060
They're remarkable devices.

40
00:03:08,061 --> 00:03:11,060
They're making more horsepower than we've ever made before.

41
00:03:11,061 --> 00:03:14,300
they start perfectly, and they hardly produce any pollution

42
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:16,560
at all.

43
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:19,460
Because of electronics, you can almost seal the hood today

44
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:21,000
and run the car for 100,000 miles.

45
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,500
Future gas-powered engines will be even more reliable,

46
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:31,120
and engineers are making progress on high-tech, zero-emission

47
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:32,020
alternatives.

48
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:39,160
It's taken over 100 years of driving and a billion engines

49
00:03:39,161 --> 00:03:40,558
engines to get here.

50
00:03:40,559 --> 00:03:45,100
People have tinkered and tested to make cars run and to make them

51
00:03:45,101 --> 00:03:52,100
fun. But coming up with the first practical engine was the biggest challenge of all.

52
00:03:52,101 --> 00:03:59,100
In 1878, Nicholas Otto of Germany built a single cylinder power plant that was smaller

53
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:03,638
and stronger than others around the world.

54
00:04:03,639 --> 00:04:06,820
His engine generated more power than bulkier

55
00:04:06,821 --> 00:04:11,060
rivals thanks to two additional steps.

56
00:04:11,061 --> 00:04:14,220
This motion came to be known as a four-stroke cycle,

57
00:04:14,221 --> 00:04:16,920
the same system still used in cars today.

58
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,620
During the intake phase, fuel and air

59
00:04:22,621 --> 00:04:26,520
are drawn into the cylinder as the piston moves down.

60
00:04:26,521 --> 00:04:28,580
In compression, the piston moves up

61
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:32,960
to squeeze the fuel-air mixture, making it more explosive.

62
00:04:32,961 --> 00:04:36,160
Then ignition occurs, which hurls the piston down,

63
00:04:36,161 --> 00:04:38,720
creating the power stroke.

64
00:04:38,721 --> 00:04:41,320
During the final stage, the piston moves back up

65
00:04:41,321 --> 00:04:43,100
to exhaust any unburned gases.

66
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:49,180
Earlier two-cycle models that skipped compression

67
00:04:49,181 --> 00:04:52,820
and ignored exhaust were left in the dust.

68
00:04:52,821 --> 00:04:54,980
Four cycles packed a bigger punch

69
00:04:54,981 --> 00:04:56,260
with the same amount of fuel.

70
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:03,160
By 1886, Gottlieb Daimler and Carl Benz

71
00:05:03,161 --> 00:05:06,400
improved on their countryman's tabletop design.

72
00:05:06,401 --> 00:05:10,300
Gasoline-powered vehicles were on the road.

73
00:05:10,301 --> 00:05:13,160
These engines were better built.

74
00:05:13,161 --> 00:05:15,780
They were more carefully machined.

75
00:05:15,781 --> 00:05:19,600
They had better compression, had better horsepower.

76
00:05:19,601 --> 00:05:22,340
They began to use gasoline.

77
00:05:22,341 --> 00:05:26,680
The other vehicles used a mixture of camphor and gasoline

78
00:05:26,681 --> 00:05:29,860
and sometimes kerosene.

79
00:05:29,861 --> 00:05:31,960
They worked, but they had little to fear

80
00:05:31,961 --> 00:05:35,800
from early police with top speeds under 10 miles per hour.

81
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:41,060
The Americans were a little behind their European

82
00:05:41,061 --> 00:05:44,360
counterparts at first, but they caught up with a vengeance

83
00:05:44,361 --> 00:05:47,080
by the end of the century.

84
00:05:47,081 --> 00:05:50,500
The Durye brothers built one of the earliest successful gas

85
00:05:50,501 --> 00:05:55,420
powered vehicles in the US in 1895.

86
00:05:55,421 --> 00:05:58,120
The Durye was a one cylinder engine.

87
00:05:58,121 --> 00:06:00,300
It was somewhere around four horsepower.

88
00:06:00,301 --> 00:06:06,140
propel the vehicle 0, 9 or 10 miles an hour going downhill

89
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:09,140
with a tailwind.

90
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:11,840
By the turn of the century, gas engines became more

91
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:15,540
powerful as two-cylinder models grew more common.

92
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:19,960
But early drivers still had other options.

93
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:24,460
The first national automobile show was held in 1900 and they

94
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:29,900
had a People's Choice voting award and surprisingly

95
00:06:29,901 --> 00:06:31,900
the electric was the choice of the people.

96
00:06:31,901 --> 00:06:35,900
Steam was second, and the gasoline was a distant third

97
00:06:35,901 --> 00:06:41,900
because the gasoline was noisy, smelly, and it was dirty.

98
00:06:41,901 --> 00:06:43,900
But just after the turn of the century,

99
00:06:43,901 --> 00:06:46,900
internal combustion engines started taking over.

100
00:06:46,901 --> 00:06:49,900
They were more reliable and had better range and power

101
00:06:49,901 --> 00:06:52,900
thanks to multiple cylinders.

102
00:06:52,901 --> 00:06:55,900
The disadvantages of steam and electricity

103
00:06:55,901 --> 00:06:58,900
became harder to live with.

104
00:06:58,901 --> 00:07:03,400
The gasoline engine won out over the others' steam and

105
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:07,700
electricity 100 years ago for the very simple reason it was

106
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,140
the best engine.

107
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:10,840
The gasoline engine, among other things,

108
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:14,640
can carry its own fuel with it, which an electric can't.

109
00:07:14,740 --> 00:07:18,860
It starts up immediately, which a steam engine doesn't.

110
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:22,660
And it's fairly simple.

111
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:25,360
The epitome of the simple, reliable, rugged,

112
00:07:25,361 --> 00:07:30,760
rugged gasoline-powered car appeared in 1908.

113
00:07:30,761 --> 00:07:35,680
That was the year Henry Ford introduced his Model T.

114
00:07:35,681 --> 00:07:38,840
When the Model T came out, it was a sensation.

115
00:07:38,841 --> 00:07:43,240
It originally cost $850, which was not cheap.

116
00:07:43,241 --> 00:07:47,280
But for your $850, you got a four-cylinder car that was

117
00:07:47,281 --> 00:07:48,740
relatively powerful.

118
00:07:48,741 --> 00:07:51,060
You got a very modern engine.

119
00:07:51,061 --> 00:07:55,080
You got a very strong, light car that could navigate

120
00:07:55,081 --> 00:07:58,680
the abysmal American roads, and the Model T very quickly

121
00:07:58,681 --> 00:08:01,780
became the best-selling car in America, and eventually the

122
00:08:01,781 --> 00:08:04,620
best-selling car in the world.

123
00:08:04,621 --> 00:08:07,920
Its 20-horsepower, four-cylinder engine was more

124
00:08:07,921 --> 00:08:11,400
powerful, yet lighter, than other engines at the time.

125
00:08:11,401 --> 00:08:15,100
That's because Ford cast the engine block all in one piece,

126
00:08:15,101 --> 00:08:19,800
instead of several pieces as other companies were doing.

127
00:08:19,801 --> 00:08:23,440
By 1910, long six- and eight-cylinder monsters

128
00:08:23,441 --> 00:08:24,780
began to appear.

129
00:08:24,781 --> 00:08:27,280
Speeds topped 50 miles an hour.

130
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:33,820
Moving from one cylinder to multiple cylinders

131
00:08:33,821 --> 00:08:34,820
wasn't too tough.

132
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:38,780
Workers simply attached each piston to the crankshaft

133
00:08:38,781 --> 00:08:40,920
with connecting rods.

134
00:08:40,921 --> 00:08:43,660
More cylinders turned the crankshaft faster,

135
00:08:43,661 --> 00:08:45,760
which pushed you down the road faster.

136
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:51,500
Air and fuel were still mixed in the carburetor,

137
00:08:51,501 --> 00:08:53,560
just like earlier single cylinder engines.

138
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:57,620
But multiple cylinders needed an extra link

139
00:08:57,621 --> 00:08:59,660
between the carburetor and each cylinder,

140
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:02,600
a part known as the intake manifold.

141
00:09:06,700 --> 00:09:09,620
Pushing the accelerator opened the throttle valve

142
00:09:09,621 --> 00:09:10,960
at the top of the carburetor,

143
00:09:10,961 --> 00:09:13,000
which allowed air to flow through.

144
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:16,140
As air rushed into the throat of the carburetor,

145
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:19,540
it pulled small droplets of fuel from a fuel bowl.

146
00:09:19,541 --> 00:09:23,540
This combustible mixture entered the intake manifold.

147
00:09:23,541 --> 00:09:29,540
Small tunnels in the manifold channeled an equal amount of the mixture to each cylinder.

148
00:09:29,541 --> 00:09:35,540
After combustion, the exhaust left the cylinders through similar tunnels in the exhaust manifold,

149
00:09:35,541 --> 00:09:41,540
which led to a single exhaust pipe.

150
00:09:41,541 --> 00:09:47,540
Then in 1915, Henry Leland's Cadillac Company became the first manufacturer

151
00:09:47,541 --> 00:09:49,940
to mass-produce eight-cylinder engines

152
00:09:49,941 --> 00:09:53,180
arranged in a V-shape.

153
00:09:53,181 --> 00:09:55,440
Angling the cylinders towards each other

154
00:09:55,441 --> 00:09:58,080
allowed this powerful engine to take up less space

155
00:09:58,081 --> 00:10:03,180
under the hood than a long row of side-by-side cylinders.

156
00:10:03,181 --> 00:10:06,000
It was really a marvel of technology.

157
00:10:06,001 --> 00:10:10,000
It was 314 cubic inches, 77 horsepower,

158
00:10:10,001 --> 00:10:12,400
pushed our cars upwards of 70 miles an hour.

159
00:10:12,401 --> 00:10:15,700
The V8 design also offered reliability and smoothness

160
00:10:15,701 --> 00:10:18,660
that was unheard of in four-cylinder and six-cylinder

161
00:10:18,661 --> 00:10:19,361
engines.

162
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:25,540
314 cubic inches was the total volume

163
00:10:25,541 --> 00:10:28,540
inside the eight cylinders.

164
00:10:28,541 --> 00:10:31,140
Power increased as the cubic inches increased,

165
00:10:31,141 --> 00:10:33,760
since a larger cylinder provided more room

166
00:10:33,761 --> 00:10:35,060
for a stronger boom.

167
00:10:38,660 --> 00:10:43,300
Then in 1930, Cadillac came out with a real beast of an engine,

168
00:10:43,301 --> 00:10:48,900
A massive 452 cubic inch V16.

169
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,600
Its power and 100 mile an hour speed

170
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:56,180
soon attracted some very interesting customers.

171
00:10:56,280 --> 00:10:59,880
It attracted people such as Al Capone and Machine Gun Kelly.

172
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:02,360
We're talking about a day when there were not radio

173
00:11:02,460 --> 00:11:04,660
transmissions in the police cars or helicopters.

174
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:08,060
So it became a matter of who had the fastest horse

175
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:09,360
to get out of town.

176
00:11:09,460 --> 00:11:13,300
And some of the Cadillacs fit the bill very well.

177
00:11:13,301 --> 00:11:16,800
Although Cadillac made the V16 until 1940,

178
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:20,100
the depression definitely depressed luxury car makers

179
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:22,100
in the 1930s.

180
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:27,140
Getting back to basics set the stage for Henry Ford once again.

181
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:31,660
In the 1920s, V8s only appeared in luxury cars.

182
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:37,460
But by 1932, Ford managed to offer V8s in his lower-priced cars.

183
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:40,060
Just like the four-cylinder Model T,

184
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:42,700
casting the V8 engine block in one piece

185
00:11:42,701 --> 00:11:46,640
was the secret to its affordability.

186
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:52,680
The Ford V8 became the muscle car of the 1930s and the 1940s.

187
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:58,480
And in fact, John Dillinger used Ford V8s very frequently.

188
00:11:58,580 --> 00:12:00,460
Dillinger never really purchased cars.

189
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:01,880
He stole them.

190
00:12:01,980 --> 00:12:06,260
But he once wrote a letter to Henry Ford that states

191
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:10,000
that when I'm stealing a car, I always look for a Ford

192
00:12:10,001 --> 00:12:13,000
Ford because it's the best car and the fastest car

193
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:13,800
in the business.

194
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:19,240
But not all engine innovations worked.

195
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:22,380
In the early 1940s, some consumer car makers

196
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:25,580
went a little overboard by incorporating an idea

197
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:28,220
that race cars had been using to boost their power

198
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:32,060
for many years, dual carburetors.

199
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:35,160
Two carburetors allowed more fuel and air into the engine,

200
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:38,120
leading to lots of power.

201
00:12:38,121 --> 00:12:40,680
But coordinating the flow of air was difficult,

202
00:12:40,681 --> 00:12:43,820
and most cars stuck with the single carburetor engines.

203
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:49,060
In the first 50 years of the century,

204
00:12:49,061 --> 00:12:51,360
engine designers had come a long way,

205
00:12:51,361 --> 00:12:55,680
from one-cylinder wimps to multi-cylinder monsters.

206
00:12:55,681 --> 00:12:58,880
But things would get a lot tougher in the next 50 years,

207
00:12:58,881 --> 00:13:01,680
as consumers demanded more power,

208
00:13:01,681 --> 00:13:04,680
and the government laid down the law on pollution.

209
00:13:08,120 --> 00:13:14,120
James Watt invented the term horsepower by watching ponies lift coal from mine shacks.

210
00:13:14,121 --> 00:13:18,830
One horsepower equals 330 pounds raised 100 feet in one minute.

211
00:13:18,831 --> 00:13:22,120
The Body Shop will return on Modern Marvels.

212
00:13:27,240 --> 00:13:30,520
We now return to The Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

213
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:37,640
Engines, explosions and flying metal. No wonder we think they're so cool.

214
00:13:37,641 --> 00:13:43,800
But obviously, there has to be some way of controlling all this action, and that is what the camshaft is for.

215
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:46,520
Think of it as the engine's mechanical brain.

216
00:13:46,940 --> 00:13:52,340
It's located right up here and connected to the crankshaft down here by this timing chain.

217
00:13:52,341 --> 00:13:59,480
As the camshaft spins around, these lobes open and close each cylinder valve at just the right time.

218
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:05,820
All of this split-second precision adds up to a steady source of power and a nice smooth ride.

219
00:14:07,640 --> 00:14:18,653
Following the end of World War II in 1945, engineers kept squeezing more power and smoothness out of engines by making V8s better, instead of just building bigger and

220
00:14:18,654 --> 00:14:19,980
bigger power plants.

221
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:31,820
The next real development was in the high compression overhead valve V8, which Oldsmobile and Cadillac introduced in 1948.

222
00:14:31,821 --> 00:14:43,797
And high compression simply meant, if you could think of it in terms of keeping the top on the teapot as it's boiling, it's holding that pressure in there to make it

223
00:14:43,798 --> 00:14:44,498
work.

224
00:14:45,240 --> 00:14:49,320
It improved significantly the power to weight ratio of the cars.

225
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:55,900
The overhead valve engine was a full 200 pounds lighter weight than the flathead that it replaced.

226
00:14:55,901 --> 00:15:03,660
A lighter engine and a lighter car in general led to more speed or better gas mileage.

227
00:15:04,780 --> 00:15:07,200
Lower weight meant less for the engine to push.

228
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:19,220
One way of doing this was by using lighter, stronger metal alloys, like aluminum pistons, which popped up in the late 40s.

229
00:15:19,221 --> 00:15:28,060
In 1960, Buick and Oldsmobile continued the light is right mantra with engines made almost

230
00:15:28,061 --> 00:15:29,240
entirely of aluminum.

231
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:37,620
But as oil and gas prices continued to rise in the late 60s and early 70s, four and six

232
00:15:37,621 --> 00:15:39,400
cylinder engines began to make a comeback.

233
00:15:40,980 --> 00:15:44,440
Fuel mileage rose, but performance fizzled.

234
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,800
Customers wanted more power from their economy cars.

235
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:54,220
At the same time, the government wanted cleaner running cars.

236
00:15:54,221 --> 00:15:57,220
This would sap even more strength.

237
00:15:57,221 --> 00:16:00,220
Engine designers faced an incredible challenge.

238
00:16:00,221 --> 00:16:07,220
Their solution was to replace the carburetor with a new system known as fuel injection.

239
00:16:13,220 --> 00:16:16,220
This is a carburetor.

240
00:16:16,221 --> 00:16:23,580
It became a thing of the past by the 1970s when it was replaced by something smaller, lighter, and more efficient.

241
00:16:24,180 --> 00:16:27,040
The fuel injector. It's standard on most cars today.

242
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:28,600
Now, here's how it works.

243
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:34,440
This nozzle here sends fuel into the cylinder in a fine, precise spray.

244
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:38,380
You get a better mixture with the air and more bang for your buck.

245
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:44,340
In other words, more power, more mileage, and less pollution from the same amount of fuel.

246
00:16:44,341 --> 00:16:47,340
Well, no wonder the carburetor's history.

247
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:52,560
The first fuel injectors came on the scene in the 50s,

248
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,800
like the complicated system on the 58 Corvette.

249
00:16:57,080 --> 00:17:01,380
The original fuel injection systems were mechanical systems.

250
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:03,520
They were controlled by cams and gears,

251
00:17:03,780 --> 00:17:05,440
and they were very, very expensive.

252
00:17:05,620 --> 00:17:08,720
They were very complex because these control mechanisms

253
00:17:08,721 --> 00:17:11,320
required very precise, fine machining.

254
00:17:11,321 --> 00:17:17,320
Electronic fuel injection was the answer to engine designers' prayers.

255
00:17:17,321 --> 00:17:24,320
In the early 1970s, computers gave them all of fuel injection's benefits and made it simpler

256
00:17:24,321 --> 00:17:26,320
and cheaper than mechanical systems.

257
00:17:26,321 --> 00:17:32,320
But within a few years, fuel injection still wasn't clean enough for the government.

258
00:17:32,321 --> 00:17:37,320
Emission standards grew more strict in the 1970s.

259
00:17:37,321 --> 00:17:42,640
The industry turned to the catalytic converter, which was attached to the exhaust system.

260
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:55,160
It takes some of the pollutants that come out of the engine, and by running them over a grid of platinum and other catalysts, converts them into less harmful materials.

261
00:17:55,800 --> 00:17:59,860
And that all of a sudden made it possible to meet much tighter emission standards.

262
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:07,240
But in the late 70s, even fuel injection and the catalytic converter couldn't meet government standards.

263
00:18:07,320 --> 00:18:11,320
Electronic sensors were the next step.

264
00:18:11,321 --> 00:18:14,320
Sensors let the two systems talk to each other,

265
00:18:14,321 --> 00:18:19,320
giving engines a near-perfect fuel-air mix every time they fired.

266
00:18:19,321 --> 00:18:23,320
These days, we've taken this to a very, very fine art,

267
00:18:23,321 --> 00:18:27,320
and a modern engine produces between 1 and 2 percent as much air pollution

268
00:18:27,321 --> 00:18:30,320
as an uncontrolled engine from the 60s did.

269
00:18:30,321 --> 00:18:38,268
it. Future gas engines will deliver much more radical innovations.

270
00:18:38,269 --> 00:18:41,520
In fact, having a V8 engine

271
00:18:41,521 --> 00:18:46,720
in the future may not necessarily mean that all eight pistons are pumping all the time.

272
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:53,360
We're going to see future engines that can shut off cylinders when they're not needed,

273
00:18:53,361 --> 00:18:58,320
and that's very good from a fuel economy standpoint because typically most engines,

274
00:18:58,321 --> 00:19:00,160
when you're just cruising down the highway

275
00:19:00,161 --> 00:19:02,860
or using maybe 10% of their maximum output.

276
00:19:05,800 --> 00:19:09,360
Other car engines may not even run on gasoline at all.

277
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:13,100
One recent alternative is actually a very old idea.

278
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:16,580
Vehicles powered by electric motors

279
00:19:16,581 --> 00:19:18,340
don't produce any harmful emissions,

280
00:19:18,341 --> 00:19:21,160
but they still face some of the same limitations

281
00:19:21,161 --> 00:19:23,300
that existed 100 years ago.

282
00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:27,340
Many different manufacturers have tried electric vehicles,

283
00:19:27,341 --> 00:19:31,880
vehicles, and we've seen that they can be powerful and exciting just like an internal

284
00:19:31,881 --> 00:19:36,240
combustion engine, but they've been universally limited by range.

285
00:19:36,241 --> 00:19:41,580
So once you're in the 60 miles to 100 mile range, you're beginning to stretch what's

286
00:19:41,581 --> 00:19:43,840
practical.

287
00:19:43,841 --> 00:19:48,340
Other recently introduced consumer models are trying to have the best of both worlds

288
00:19:48,341 --> 00:19:51,140
by using two sources of power.

289
00:19:51,141 --> 00:19:54,620
These are known as hybrid vehicles.

290
00:19:54,621 --> 00:19:57,720
The Honda Insight is one example.

291
00:19:57,721 --> 00:20:01,220
It uses a small gas engine supplemented by an electric motor

292
00:20:01,221 --> 00:20:04,820
when extra power is needed.

293
00:20:04,821 --> 00:20:07,420
Since the gas engine has to do less work,

294
00:20:07,421 --> 00:20:10,920
the Insight averages about 70 miles per gallon.

295
00:20:10,921 --> 00:20:14,360
That's tops in official US government mileage measuring

296
00:20:14,361 --> 00:20:16,580
history.

297
00:20:16,581 --> 00:20:19,160
Fuel cells are currently under research

298
00:20:19,161 --> 00:20:22,200
and may become the first practical zero emission

299
00:20:22,201 --> 00:20:24,480
power plants.

300
00:20:24,481 --> 00:20:28,880
They generate power by passing hydrogen through a special membrane.

301
00:20:28,881 --> 00:20:32,320
Electrons are released, which generates electricity.

302
00:20:32,321 --> 00:20:35,820
Then the free hydrogen and the electrons reconnect

303
00:20:35,821 --> 00:20:43,040
and are combined with oxygen, leading to the reaction's only other byproduct, water.

304
00:20:43,041 --> 00:20:46,320
NASA engineers developed fuel cells like these

305
00:20:46,321 --> 00:20:52,040
to provide power on hydrogen-fueled rockets for the space program.

306
00:20:52,041 --> 00:20:54,880
Fuel cells basically have about twice the efficiency

307
00:20:54,881 --> 00:20:56,460
of an internal combustion engine.

308
00:20:56,580 --> 00:20:58,920
So again, fuel economy should be very good.

309
00:20:58,921 --> 00:21:01,420
The issue is, as they run on hydrogen,

310
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:05,540
there's not a good, practical, inexpensive way

311
00:21:05,541 --> 00:21:07,980
to either produce hydrogen today,

312
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:11,980
nor transport hydrogen, nor store it on board.

313
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:16,580
Since hydrogen gas would require a trailer-sized tank

314
00:21:16,581 --> 00:21:20,240
and low-temperature liquid hydrogen can be dangerous,

315
00:21:20,241 --> 00:21:27,600
the goal of a powerful, efficient, convenient, zero-emission engine still seems just beyond our reach.

316
00:21:30,860 --> 00:21:35,400
Roughly one out of every four U.S. patents relates to a part of the automobile,

317
00:21:35,900 --> 00:21:37,980
more than any other consumer product.

318
00:21:39,320 --> 00:21:42,780
The Body Shop will continue on Modern Marvels.

319
00:21:42,781 --> 00:21:52,640
We now return to The Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

320
00:21:55,820 --> 00:21:59,160
Now that we've looked at the heart of the car, let's look at the skin.

321
00:21:59,840 --> 00:22:04,980
The exterior of this Taurus is a combination of plastic and hardened steel.

322
00:22:06,200 --> 00:22:10,880
In some places, the steel measures an unbelievably thin three-quarters of a millimeter.

323
00:22:10,881 --> 00:22:12,880
Hey, Chris, let's cut out a hunk.

324
00:22:12,881 --> 00:22:13,880
Say it right here.

325
00:22:22,880 --> 00:22:24,880
Thanks, Chris.

326
00:22:24,881 --> 00:22:25,880
Now, don't worry.

327
00:22:25,881 --> 00:22:27,880
There's more than this skin protecting you.

328
00:22:27,881 --> 00:22:29,880
We'll talk about that later.

329
00:22:29,881 --> 00:22:32,880
But for everyone who thinks that beauty is only skin deep,

330
00:22:32,881 --> 00:22:36,880
the body of a car may be the perfect example.

331
00:22:36,881 --> 00:22:39,880
Over the decades, designers have shaped car bodies

332
00:22:39,881 --> 00:22:41,880
into an almost endless variety.

333
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:47,880
From ragtops and convertibles to tail fins,

334
00:22:47,881 --> 00:22:52,880
uninspiring box-like designs to sleek aerodynamic wonders,

335
00:22:52,881 --> 00:22:56,880
cars have looks to satisfy almost any taste.

336
00:22:56,881 --> 00:23:00,880
But all of this attention to form is actually rooted in function.

337
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:07,880
The earliest cars were simply wooden horse carriages with

338
00:23:07,881 --> 00:23:09,840
with motors.

339
00:23:09,841 --> 00:23:13,280
There were no doors, no roofs, not even a windshield.

340
00:23:13,281 --> 00:23:15,780
Drivers were totally exposed to the elements.

341
00:23:19,540 --> 00:23:22,700
As car speeds went up, engineers raised the sides

342
00:23:22,701 --> 00:23:24,380
and added doors.

343
00:23:24,381 --> 00:23:27,060
Windshields and carriage-like ragtop roofs

344
00:23:27,061 --> 00:23:30,940
became popular after the turn of the century.

345
00:23:30,941 --> 00:23:33,720
One of the earliest completely enclosed cars

346
00:23:33,721 --> 00:23:37,580
was the 1905 Cadillac Osceola.

347
00:23:37,581 --> 00:23:40,780
It was a one-of-a-kind, turn-of-the-century concept car.

348
00:23:41,340 --> 00:23:46,060
The closed-body idea was very appealing to Cadillac's founder, Henry Leland.

349
00:23:47,160 --> 00:23:50,600
When you look at the car, you can see it has a very tall silhouette,

350
00:23:51,160 --> 00:23:55,380
and one of the reasons for that was that Henry Leland was very tall, well over six feet tall.

351
00:23:56,020 --> 00:24:00,340
Osceola was such a significant innovation to the automobile business that Henry Leland kept that car,

352
00:24:00,800 --> 00:24:03,040
and that was his actual personal car that he drove.

353
00:24:03,041 --> 00:24:12,660
However, it took quite a while for closed-bodied cars to catch on because they were expensive.

354
00:24:12,661 --> 00:24:20,480
In fact, closed cars didn't outsell open models until 1927, 22 years later.

355
00:24:20,481 --> 00:24:24,800
In addition to offering more protection from the elements, cars were gradually changing

356
00:24:24,801 --> 00:24:29,540
their skins from wood to steel.

357
00:24:29,541 --> 00:24:32,540
The fancy steel bodies appeared as early as 1912,

358
00:24:32,541 --> 00:24:36,540
but they didn't dominate the market until the 1930s.

359
00:24:38,540 --> 00:24:44,540
Advances in metal shaping set the stage for one of the brightest periods in American car styling.

360
00:24:44,541 --> 00:24:50,540
Before the late 20s, more practical engineers had always dictated a car's shape.

361
00:24:50,541 --> 00:24:58,540
That changed in 1927, when General Motors placed Harley Earl in charge of the art and color section.

362
00:24:58,541 --> 00:25:01,860
the first department in the industry dedicated exclusively

363
00:25:01,861 --> 00:25:05,060
to styling.

364
00:25:05,061 --> 00:25:09,160
His first GM design was the 1927 LaSalle,

365
00:25:09,161 --> 00:25:14,440
which became the pace car for that year's Indianapolis 500.

366
00:25:14,441 --> 00:25:17,720
The styling on the car is very fresh.

367
00:25:17,721 --> 00:25:19,860
It has flowing lines, whereas if you

368
00:25:19,861 --> 00:25:21,600
look at the earlier cars that were designed

369
00:25:21,601 --> 00:25:24,320
by the mechanical engineers, they're very straightforward,

370
00:25:24,321 --> 00:25:26,060
more form and function than styling.

371
00:25:28,540 --> 00:25:35,360
Flowing lines came from a process that Earl used over the decades at GM, his idea of clay modeling.

372
00:25:36,520 --> 00:25:38,560
Previously, models had been made of wood.

373
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:43,340
Clay gave Earl the freedom to test many different shapes and ideas.

374
00:25:44,180 --> 00:25:46,700
It's an innovation that's still in use today.

375
00:25:50,560 --> 00:25:58,360
In 1929, the Kord Car Company hired Gordon Buhrig to supervise the design of its Duesenberg line of cars.

376
00:25:58,540 --> 00:26:03,740
Gordon Buig was really one of the finest designers then.

377
00:26:03,741 --> 00:26:07,680
He could design any vehicle and leave off all the trim.

378
00:26:08,020 --> 00:26:09,520
It would still be beautiful.

379
00:26:10,100 --> 00:26:13,020
It was beautiful in shape, in form.

380
00:26:13,660 --> 00:26:16,820
And that's what made his design so ascending.

381
00:26:18,920 --> 00:26:22,820
Movie stars like Clark Gable, Gary Cooper, and Mae West

382
00:26:22,821 --> 00:26:26,800
happily paid about $15,000 for their doozies,

383
00:26:26,801 --> 00:26:30,240
or $153,000 today.

384
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:34,900
With that kind of price tag, it's a good thing the J model is still considered

385
00:26:34,901 --> 00:26:39,000
one of the most stylish and opulent cars in American automotive history.

386
00:26:42,360 --> 00:26:48,260
Although the depression sank luxury car sales, advances in body design continued.

387
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:56,560
The 1934 Chrysler Airflow was aptly named since it was the first car designed in a wind tunnel.

388
00:26:56,800 --> 00:27:03,800
This allowed the car to slice through the air more efficiently than earlier models.

389
00:27:06,800 --> 00:27:10,698
Smaller, sporty cars became popular in the 1950s.

390
00:27:10,699 --> 00:27:13,800
In 1953, designers at Chevrolet decided

391
00:27:13,801 --> 00:27:16,002
to try out a non-steel body.

392
00:27:16,003 --> 00:27:20,800
They used a new type of plastic known as fiberglass to create

393
00:27:20,801 --> 00:27:23,340
to create the highly successful Corvette.

394
00:27:24,680 --> 00:27:26,580
Fiberglass made the Corvette light

395
00:27:26,581 --> 00:27:28,260
and allowed for stylish curves,

396
00:27:28,580 --> 00:27:30,720
but there was also a practical reason

397
00:27:30,721 --> 00:27:31,920
for using this material.

398
00:27:33,900 --> 00:27:36,720
It's cheaper to build a low-volume car

399
00:27:36,721 --> 00:27:37,700
out of fiberglass,

400
00:27:38,160 --> 00:27:42,020
and the reason is when you make a body out of steel,

401
00:27:42,380 --> 00:27:44,660
you have to produce these enormous dies

402
00:27:44,661 --> 00:27:47,300
that stamp a flat sheet of steel

403
00:27:47,301 --> 00:27:50,280
and produce a fender or a hood or a roof.

404
00:27:50,800 --> 00:27:56,800
The Corvette's fiberglass body was molded on less expensive equipment.

405
00:27:56,801 --> 00:28:03,800
Since annual Corvette production has always been in the tens of thousands compared to hundreds of thousands for more common models,

406
00:28:03,801 --> 00:28:09,800
Chevrolet saved manufacturing costs with this non-steel body.

407
00:28:09,801 --> 00:28:14,800
But fiberglass also helped the Corvette seem new and different.

408
00:28:14,801 --> 00:28:21,800
However, 1970s car bodies offered very little in new and different styles.

409
00:28:21,801 --> 00:28:25,800
Designers were being pulled in several directions.

410
00:28:25,801 --> 00:28:31,800
Safety standards from the government started to prohibit any sharp corners on car bodies.

411
00:28:31,801 --> 00:28:35,800
At the same time, the government was requiring higher fuel economy,

412
00:28:35,801 --> 00:28:39,800
which increased the need for smaller, lighter cars.

413
00:28:39,801 --> 00:28:44,800
But customers didn't want to give up any interior space.

414
00:28:44,900 --> 00:28:47,440
So if you want to make the outside of the car smaller,

415
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:50,440
keep the inside big, well, you make a box.

416
00:28:50,540 --> 00:28:53,480
And by the time we got to the late 70s,

417
00:28:53,580 --> 00:28:56,020
we had cars with very little chrome on them,

418
00:28:56,120 --> 00:29:01,380
very few sharp edges, and extremely boxy shapes.

419
00:29:01,480 --> 00:29:03,560
In recent years, some designers

420
00:29:03,660 --> 00:29:06,300
have looked to aluminum as the next body material

421
00:29:06,400 --> 00:29:08,500
to unseat steel.

422
00:29:08,501 --> 00:29:14,400
Although aluminum is lighter, it's not as easy to shape as steel, and since different

423
00:29:14,401 --> 00:29:19,780
metals can't be welded together, it cannot be attached to steel, which is still the most

424
00:29:19,781 --> 00:29:25,060
common material for the underlying structure of the car.

425
00:29:25,061 --> 00:29:29,460
Due to these limitations, some manufacturers are starting to make certain body panels out

426
00:29:29,461 --> 00:29:33,900
of plastic and keep other parts steel.

427
00:29:33,901 --> 00:29:38,500
Future stylists may be working with carbon fiber materials.

428
00:29:38,501 --> 00:29:44,460
carbon fiber combines durable plastics and carbon compounds to create a lightweight material

429
00:29:44,461 --> 00:29:47,674
up to 35 times stronger than steel.

430
00:29:47,675 --> 00:29:52,540
Indy car bodies are made from this advanced material.

431
00:29:52,541 --> 00:29:58,280
But with a price tag 10 to 100 times higher than steel, it might stay on the track and

432
00:29:58,281 --> 00:30:03,491
out of John Q. Public's garage for years to come.

433
00:30:03,492 --> 00:30:07,320
Along with the skin, every car has a

434
00:30:07,321 --> 00:30:12,840
skeleton. Now this torus has a unitized body, that's a fancy term for a big cage

435
00:30:12,841 --> 00:30:19,440
just underneath the exterior. It consists of the roof, six pillars in between and

436
00:30:19,441 --> 00:30:23,640
at either end of the doors, and the undercarriage. It's the thickest,

437
00:30:23,641 --> 00:30:28,380
strongest part of a car. It supports everything else like the engine, the

438
00:30:28,381 --> 00:30:33,600
transmission, and the wheels. And during collisions, its design protects

439
00:30:33,601 --> 00:30:39,480
passengers by actually absorbing impact.

440
00:30:39,481 --> 00:30:46,640
Early cars didn't have unitized bodies, just metal ladder-shaped structures known as chassis.

441
00:30:46,641 --> 00:30:55,980
A strong chassis kept bad roads from making mincemeat out of the rest of the car.

442
00:30:55,981 --> 00:31:01,720
In the 1920s, workers joined the chassis and the body near the end of the assembly line.

443
00:31:01,721 --> 00:31:05,720
But that began to change in the 1930s.

444
00:31:05,721 --> 00:31:10,720
In 1936, Lincoln came out with a car called a Zephyr,

445
00:31:10,721 --> 00:31:13,720
and it was what we would think of today as a unit body.

446
00:31:13,721 --> 00:31:19,720
That is, the chassis and the body were actually put together as a unit.

447
00:31:19,721 --> 00:31:26,720
The unit, or unitized body, could be made lighter than two separate pieces.

448
00:31:26,721 --> 00:31:31,600
It was also quieter since it was welded together instead of attached with rivets.

449
00:31:32,300 --> 00:31:38,080
These factors were important as America's roads became smoother and the cars traveled faster.

450
00:31:40,580 --> 00:31:48,400
In the 60s and 70s, small cars generally used unit bodies, and larger cars and light trucks used chassis.

451
00:31:50,020 --> 00:31:55,240
A mixture still exists today since each type of construction has pros and cons.

452
00:31:55,241 --> 00:32:01,180
For instance, a unit body can be safer in an accident since it's all in one piece,

453
00:32:01,181 --> 00:32:03,400
but harder to repair afterwards.

454
00:32:03,401 --> 00:32:14,140
Chassis suppliers are also starting to make things easier for car manufacturers.

455
00:32:14,141 --> 00:32:20,560
Instead of just sending car makers a metal frame, some are sending more complete undercarriages.

456
00:32:20,561 --> 00:32:25,560
This concept is known as the rolling chassis.

457
00:32:25,660 --> 00:32:27,560
You'd find complete wheel systems.

458
00:32:27,660 --> 00:32:30,200
You'd find the axle, the driveline.

459
00:32:30,300 --> 00:32:33,000
You'd find all the plumbing that goes on a vehicle.

460
00:32:33,100 --> 00:32:34,800
You'd take it to the assembler.

461
00:32:34,900 --> 00:32:39,620
They'd drop an engine in, put the seats and the cabin box,

462
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:41,820
and go.

463
00:32:41,920 --> 00:32:44,420
Just like automobile skins, designers

464
00:32:44,520 --> 00:32:46,320
are working with a variety of materials

465
00:32:46,420 --> 00:32:50,560
to make the underlying frame stronger and lighter.

466
00:32:50,561 --> 00:32:54,560
Steel still rules, but aluminum and carbon fiber composite

467
00:32:54,660 --> 00:33:00,000
materials may soon make their way into the car's skeleton.

468
00:33:00,100 --> 00:33:03,600
But the frame won't go anywhere unless the rubber meets the

469
00:33:03,700 --> 00:33:04,500
road.

470
00:33:04,600 --> 00:33:07,520
Coming up, an inside look at the world of wheels.

471
00:33:10,620 --> 00:33:14,320
In the early 1900s, an inventor attached a fake horse neck and

472
00:33:14,420 --> 00:33:17,920
head to the front of his car to put real horses at ease.

473
00:33:17,921 --> 00:33:20,920
The loud engine noises still terrified them.

474
00:33:22,920 --> 00:33:25,920
The Body Shop will return on Modern Marvels.

475
00:33:30,920 --> 00:33:34,920
We now return to The Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

476
00:33:39,920 --> 00:33:40,920
More parts, huh?

477
00:33:40,921 --> 00:33:41,920
That's right.

478
00:33:41,921 --> 00:33:42,621
That's right.

479
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:48,620
If there's a Rodney Dangerfield of car parts,

480
00:33:48,740 --> 00:33:49,880
it's got to be the wheels.

481
00:33:50,200 --> 00:33:51,480
They just don't get no respect.

482
00:33:52,040 --> 00:33:55,480
In fact, people used to test them by kicking the rubber.

483
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:57,920
And most times, you only think about them

484
00:33:57,921 --> 00:34:00,380
when something is wrong, like having to change a flat.

485
00:34:00,720 --> 00:34:01,960
But they're awfully important.

486
00:34:02,380 --> 00:34:04,120
They're the only thing touching the road.

487
00:34:04,780 --> 00:34:07,980
There are two basic parts, the metal rim and the rubber tire.

488
00:34:08,520 --> 00:34:09,720
Let's go inside this tire.

489
00:34:09,721 --> 00:34:12,720
It's made of a couple of layers or plies.

490
00:34:12,721 --> 00:34:18,720
Rubber is mixed with lots of different cord materials like nylon and steel wire strands.

491
00:34:18,721 --> 00:34:24,720
In fact, everyday run-of-the-mill tires are made of over 200 different ingredients and materials.

492
00:34:26,720 --> 00:34:31,720
Early tires didn't have fancy layers. They didn't even have air in them.

493
00:34:31,721 --> 00:34:36,720
These smooth, solid rubber tires were a pain in the neck and back for early drivers.

494
00:34:36,721 --> 00:34:40,480
drivers. It just shook the heck out of the

495
00:34:40,481 --> 00:34:41,295
automobile.

496
00:34:41,296 --> 00:34:47,220
And even though they could get the thing going, if they got out into the country

497
00:34:47,221 --> 00:34:51,010
and it rained, you couldn't get any traction.

498
00:34:51,011 --> 00:34:54,800
Tires got all pumped up after the turn of the

499
00:34:54,801 --> 00:34:55,501
century.

500
00:34:55,502 --> 00:35:02,060
The air cushion added a lot of comfort, but the typical tire only lasted for about 2,000

501
00:35:02,061 --> 00:35:02,761
miles.

502
00:35:02,762 --> 00:35:11,100
A young Harvey Firestone was one of the first proponents of these air-filled or pneumatic

503
00:35:11,101 --> 00:35:11,801
tires.

504
00:35:11,802 --> 00:35:17,440
He struck up a long-time friendship with Henry Ford before the turn of the century.

505
00:35:18,580 --> 00:35:24,460
When Firestone learned of the large number of Model T's Ford was planning to produce in 1908,

506
00:35:24,461 --> 00:35:32,040
he saw an opportunity. Harvey Firestone developed one line of his tires to fit

507
00:35:32,041 --> 00:35:37,980
on Ford's Model T cars, convinced his old friend that these tires would work for him,

508
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:42,900
and they struck up a relationship that lasted really through most of the 20th century.

509
00:35:44,960 --> 00:35:48,900
By 1910, treads gave early drivers a better grip.

510
00:35:51,400 --> 00:35:56,520
And in 1920, tire makers began placing inner tubes inside tires.

511
00:35:57,260 --> 00:36:00,980
The inner tube provided an extra layer of protection against punctures.

512
00:36:02,040 --> 00:36:09,040
Durability increased to 8,000 miles, but a set of four tires could set you back $60,

513
00:36:09,041 --> 00:36:12,040
or about one month's pay for many workers.

514
00:36:15,040 --> 00:36:22,040
More durable synthetic rubbers were developed in the 1930s, but they weren't good enough to make tires from.

515
00:36:23,040 --> 00:36:27,040
As World War II began, this task became much more critical.

516
00:36:27,041 --> 00:36:28,480
critical.

517
00:36:28,580 --> 00:36:31,480
Tires were needed for a huge number of military vehicles

518
00:36:31,580 --> 00:36:33,780
and airplanes.

519
00:36:33,880 --> 00:36:37,380
The army couldn't move without tires.

520
00:36:37,480 --> 00:36:42,680
A task force was formed to try to find the exact ingredients

521
00:36:42,780 --> 00:36:47,400
that would allow mass production of synthetic tires.

522
00:36:47,500 --> 00:36:51,540
This was the government deemed the most important project

523
00:36:51,640 --> 00:36:53,600
of the war, even more important than the development

524
00:36:53,700 --> 00:36:54,900
of the atomic bomb.

525
00:36:54,901 --> 00:37:02,420
Had we not gotten synthetic rubber, we might not have gotten to the point that we could have saved ourselves with the atomic bomb.

526
00:37:04,040 --> 00:37:09,740
After the war, tire makers mixed synthetic and natural rubbers to create more durable tires.

527
00:37:11,220 --> 00:37:15,120
Even today, natural rubber remains an important ingredient.

528
00:37:17,340 --> 00:37:22,280
Post-war consumers liked durability, but they wanted their tires to look good, too.

529
00:37:22,281 --> 00:37:27,280
White sidewalls lent an air of sophistication to these round black donuts.

530
00:37:27,281 --> 00:37:32,758
Underneath the fancy white walls, tougher rubber allowed B.F.

531
00:37:32,759 --> 00:37:36,080
Goodrich to introduce a tubeless tire

532
00:37:36,081 --> 00:37:36,795
in 1948.

533
00:37:36,796 --> 00:37:44,120
Unlike the earliest air-filled tires, it was not a round circle of inflated rubber

534
00:37:44,121 --> 00:37:50,080
placed on top of a rim. The tubeless tire consisted of only a tread and sidewalls.

535
00:37:50,081 --> 00:37:55,900
air was sealed between the sides of the tire and the rim. This allowed makers to

536
00:37:55,901 --> 00:38:04,300
toss the tube. Fewer materials meant cheaper tires. The plies or layers of the

537
00:38:04,301 --> 00:38:09,880
tires were also becoming more durable. Earlier tires had used layers of natural

538
00:38:09,881 --> 00:38:18,280
materials like cotton. Synthetic materials like rayon helped tires last longer.

539
00:38:18,281 --> 00:38:25,720
The plies inside the tires overlapped each other in a diagonal pattern known as the bias

540
00:38:25,721 --> 00:38:26,720
ply.

541
00:38:26,721 --> 00:38:30,740
But this pattern had its drawbacks.

542
00:38:30,741 --> 00:38:34,280
They looked very similar to the tires we know today, but the construction underneath

543
00:38:34,281 --> 00:38:35,860
was very different.

544
00:38:35,861 --> 00:38:39,960
Those layers, those diagonal layers, tended to wear against one another and tended to

545
00:38:39,961 --> 00:38:43,620
cause the tire to wear out sooner.

546
00:38:43,621 --> 00:38:48,940
The French Michelin Company invented the radial tire in 1946.

547
00:38:48,941 --> 00:38:54,820
It found increasing use in Europe in the 1950s and 60s, but didn't arrive in America until

548
00:38:54,821 --> 00:38:57,240
the 1970s.

549
00:38:57,241 --> 00:39:03,920
The plies in a radial tire were laid at a 90-degree angle to the grooves in the tread.

550
00:39:03,921 --> 00:39:08,420
This arrangement, along with the advances in ply materials such as fiberglass and steel

551
00:39:08,421 --> 00:39:15,420
wires resulted in tires that lasted for about 50,000 miles.

552
00:39:15,421 --> 00:39:16,121
U.S.

553
00:39:16,122 --> 00:39:22,420
tire companies were hesitant to adopt radials since rearranging the plies required

554
00:39:22,421 --> 00:39:29,580
billions of dollars worth of new manufacturing equipment.

555
00:39:29,581 --> 00:39:36,580
Radials were better, but car makers in Detroit had always put price above performance.

556
00:39:36,581 --> 00:39:40,620
Over the years, it has become a cliche that all Detroit wanted

557
00:39:40,621 --> 00:39:44,280
was tires that were round, black, and cheap,

558
00:39:44,281 --> 00:39:47,720
and they really didn't care about the round and black part.

559
00:39:53,220 --> 00:39:55,860
In recent years, car manufacturers have accepted

560
00:39:55,861 --> 00:39:59,340
that higher quality tires lead to increased gas mileage

561
00:39:59,341 --> 00:40:00,340
and better safety.

562
00:40:03,140 --> 00:40:05,840
Convenience is increasing too.

563
00:40:05,841 --> 00:40:11,160
In fact, some products on the market now may already be turning spare tires into a thing

564
00:40:11,161 --> 00:40:13,920
of the past.

565
00:40:13,921 --> 00:40:18,620
Over the decades, tires have needed less and less air pressure to remain inflated as the

566
00:40:18,621 --> 00:40:23,280
supporting structures inside have become stronger.

567
00:40:23,281 --> 00:40:29,280
Goodyear's Run Flat tire has taken that concept to the next level.

568
00:40:29,281 --> 00:40:34,920
It can be driven for 50 miles without any air pressure inside.

569
00:40:34,921 --> 00:40:38,560
The drill is sold separately.

570
00:40:38,561 --> 00:40:48,520
But even the best set of tires won't do you much good if you can't get them to stop turning.

571
00:40:48,521 --> 00:40:51,100
Now what would you say is the most forceful part of a car?

572
00:40:51,101 --> 00:40:54,160
I bet most of you said the engine, right?

573
00:40:54,161 --> 00:40:55,960
Well, you're wrong.

574
00:40:55,961 --> 00:40:57,720
It's the brakes.

575
00:40:57,721 --> 00:41:02,540
Big engines can move a car from zero to 60 in five or six seconds, but a good set of

576
00:41:02,541 --> 00:41:05,653
brakes can stop that car in only three seconds.

577
00:41:05,654 --> 00:41:08,700
Most cars today are equipped with disc brakes,

578
00:41:08,701 --> 00:41:09,980
like these here.

579
00:41:09,981 --> 00:41:15,900
The calipers pinch the brake pad against this rotor, which makes the wheel

580
00:41:15,901 --> 00:41:16,601
slow down.

581
00:41:16,602 --> 00:41:22,140
Hydraulic fluid inside this brake line makes our job of pushing the brake pedal

582
00:41:22,141 --> 00:41:26,085
a lot easier, but it wasn't always this way.

583
00:41:26,086 --> 00:41:30,300
In the 1880s, the earliest automotive inventors

584
00:41:30,301 --> 00:41:32,900
were so happy just to get their vehicles running,

585
00:41:33,280 --> 00:41:35,280
they hadn't bothered to install brakes.

586
00:41:35,840 --> 00:41:38,760
They didn't figure that they needed brakes on them.

587
00:41:39,840 --> 00:41:41,920
First of all, they didn't go fast enough.

588
00:41:43,060 --> 00:41:46,120
And what they would do was to shut the engine down

589
00:41:46,121 --> 00:41:48,160
and let the compression of the engine

590
00:41:48,161 --> 00:41:52,180
that wasn't firing any longer slow the car down.

591
00:41:54,240 --> 00:41:56,580
Consumer models in the late 1800s

592
00:41:56,581 --> 00:41:59,240
used simple friction brakes, just like wagons.

593
00:42:00,300 --> 00:42:05,020
Pulling a lever forced wood or metal to rub against the axle or rear wheel.

594
00:42:08,140 --> 00:42:11,340
As cars grew faster after the turn of the century,

595
00:42:11,341 --> 00:42:14,620
mechanical drum brakes were installed on the rear wheels only.

596
00:42:15,820 --> 00:42:20,220
The driver pulled a hand lever or pushed a foot pedal that was attached to cables.

597
00:42:23,260 --> 00:42:28,940
Tension from the cables forced two semi-circular brake shoes against the inner wall of the brake drum.

598
00:42:30,300 --> 00:42:36,760
Since the drum had been bolted to the wheel, the friction between the shoes and the drum slowed the car down.

599
00:42:40,780 --> 00:42:46,640
In the early 1920s, these cable-operated mechanical brakes spread to all four wheels.

600
00:42:48,200 --> 00:42:50,660
But the system still had limitations.

601
00:42:52,280 --> 00:42:55,220
A cable could become loose during normal driving.

602
00:42:55,221 --> 00:43:02,220
This would cause one tire to receive less braking pressure than the others, resulting in skids or spins.

603
00:43:02,221 --> 00:43:11,220
Then in 1924, Walter Chrysler's company introduced four-wheel hydraulic brakes.

604
00:43:11,221 --> 00:43:20,220
Instead of depending on cables, a hydraulic fluid system ensured that equal pressure was always distributed to all four brakes.

605
00:43:20,221 --> 00:43:22,660
brakes.

606
00:43:22,661 --> 00:43:25,720
Hydraulic fluid couldn't be compressed since there were no air

607
00:43:25,721 --> 00:43:27,080
bubbles in the thick liquid.

608
00:43:29,900 --> 00:43:33,800
There's a cylinder that's filled with hydraulic fluid.

609
00:43:33,801 --> 00:43:38,480
And when you push on a pedal, you're squirting that hydraulic

610
00:43:38,481 --> 00:43:39,640
fluid out.

611
00:43:39,641 --> 00:43:43,380
And it's relatively easy then to make sure that you're

612
00:43:43,381 --> 00:43:46,560
getting the same pressure because it's the same fluid

613
00:43:46,561 --> 00:43:48,020
going out to all four wheels.

614
00:43:50,220 --> 00:43:56,140
As cars became heavier in the next few decades, hydraulic brakes were assisted by several methods,

615
00:43:56,141 --> 00:43:59,660
such as vacuum pressure created during normal engine operation.

616
00:44:02,220 --> 00:44:07,340
Additional pressure forced through the hydraulic tubes made things easier for the driver,

617
00:44:07,341 --> 00:44:08,620
or at least for their foot.

618
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:15,580
Disc brakes filtered down to consumers from aircraft and racing cars.

619
00:44:15,581 --> 00:44:23,200
They first appeared in Europe in 1949, but didn't become popular in America until the mid-60s.

620
00:44:23,201 --> 00:44:31,060
Instead of pushing brake shoes outwards against a drum, disc brakes use pincer-like calipers

621
00:44:31,061 --> 00:44:35,165
with friction pads to squeeze a flat circular rotor.

622
00:44:35,166 --> 00:44:38,560
Like the drum in the older braking systems,

623
00:44:38,561 --> 00:44:40,420
the rotor was bolted to the wheel.

624
00:44:45,580 --> 00:44:51,900
Disc brakes helped control heat buildup, which often reduced friction or stopping power in drum brakes.

625
00:44:55,260 --> 00:44:59,100
Just as electronics helped boost engine performance in the 1970s,

626
00:44:59,660 --> 00:45:03,100
microprocessors revolutionized brakes a few years later.

627
00:45:05,180 --> 00:45:10,940
Combining disc brakes with electronic sensors and controls led to anti-lock brakes,

628
00:45:10,941 --> 00:45:14,640
also known as ABS, or Advanced Braking System.

629
00:45:16,380 --> 00:45:18,600
If you press on a brake too hard,

630
00:45:18,840 --> 00:45:21,840
you apply so much braking power that the wheel stops turning,

631
00:45:22,520 --> 00:45:25,020
and the wheel is sliding along on the pavement.

632
00:45:25,420 --> 00:45:27,140
You lose complete controllability

633
00:45:27,141 --> 00:45:32,080
because a wheel that isn't rotating has no sense of direction.

634
00:45:32,081 --> 00:45:34,980
Then the electronics engages some valves

635
00:45:34,981 --> 00:45:37,000
to reduce the hydraulic pressure

636
00:45:37,001 --> 00:45:39,320
to make sure that the brake doesn't lock up.

637
00:45:39,321 --> 00:45:46,580
And on a good system, this pulsation of brakes on, brakes off can be repeated 20, 30, 40 times a second.

638
00:45:50,180 --> 00:45:52,620
Today's ABS brakes are very sophisticated.

639
00:45:56,500 --> 00:46:04,440
But systems coming in the next few years may keep even the worst drivers out of accidents with better control and automation.

640
00:46:04,441 --> 00:46:09,440
If you get too close to the vehicle in front of you,

641
00:46:09,540 --> 00:46:12,440
audible alarms will sound to bring the situation

642
00:46:12,540 --> 00:46:13,440
to your attention.

643
00:46:15,980 --> 00:46:19,480
If you're really clueless, the car will start braking itself

644
00:46:19,580 --> 00:46:22,500
to keep a safe distance.

645
00:46:22,600 --> 00:46:26,100
It's very difficult initially to allow yourself

646
00:46:26,200 --> 00:46:29,140
to let the vehicle have control.

647
00:46:29,240 --> 00:46:31,100
You have a great urge to step on that brake

648
00:46:31,200 --> 00:46:33,440
when you see the vehicle in front of you slowing down,

649
00:46:33,441 --> 00:46:38,740
slowing down, but the system does slow you down and does keep you at a safe distance,

650
00:46:38,741 --> 00:46:41,215
and it's really quite remarkable.

651
00:46:41,216 --> 00:46:45,340
Automatic braking is bringing commuters one step closer

652
00:46:45,341 --> 00:46:50,540
to their dream of reading the paper while they drive instead of worrying about stopping and starting.

653
00:46:53,140 --> 00:46:58,380
But they'll need good suspension systems to keep their coffee in their mugs and off their laps.

654
00:46:58,381 --> 00:47:04,380
Coming up, the shocking truth of developing a smooth ride.

655
00:47:08,380 --> 00:47:13,380
Charles Goodyear was thrown in jail for a short time in 1837 when he couldn't pay for

656
00:47:13,381 --> 00:47:15,380
rubber received on credit.

657
00:47:15,381 --> 00:47:18,380
He died penniless in 1860.

658
00:47:18,381 --> 00:47:21,380
The Body Shop will return on Modern Marvels.

659
00:47:21,381 --> 00:47:35,380
Now, the Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

660
00:47:35,381 --> 00:47:42,060
Well, our shop's filling up, but we've still got a lot of parts to take off this Taurus,

661
00:47:42,061 --> 00:47:44,020
so I guess we better get back to work.

662
00:47:44,021 --> 00:47:48,920
Now, even with cushy air-filled tires and a strong frame, you'd still have a pretty

663
00:47:48,921 --> 00:47:51,437
rough ride without a suspension system.

664
00:47:51,438 --> 00:47:54,600
Springs and shock absorbers are the main parts of

665
00:47:54,601 --> 00:47:58,710
a suspension. They're the flexible link between the wheel and the frame.

666
00:47:58,711 --> 00:47:59,680
And make sure you

667
00:47:59,681 --> 00:48:03,809
don't bite your tongue off if you hit a pothole.

668
00:48:03,810 --> 00:48:07,680
Early cars were so slow there was little fear

669
00:48:07,681 --> 00:48:12,406
of tipping over. But the ride was definitely rough.

670
00:48:12,407 --> 00:48:16,020
Long, curved metal leaf springs between

671
00:48:16,021 --> 00:48:19,020
the wheels and the chassis provided some relief.

672
00:48:22,020 --> 00:48:25,520
By 1910, simple straps and early shock absorbers

673
00:48:25,521 --> 00:48:27,520
helped reduce this bouncing.

674
00:48:30,020 --> 00:48:32,520
But even as leaf springs and shock absorbers improved

675
00:48:32,521 --> 00:48:34,020
in the teens and 20s,

676
00:48:34,021 --> 00:48:37,520
drivers and passengers still had a fairly rough ride.

677
00:48:39,520 --> 00:48:43,520
Solid front and rear axles were the main culprits.

678
00:48:43,521 --> 00:48:49,120
If the left front tire dropped into a pothole, the right front tire would jerk up in the air.

679
00:48:50,120 --> 00:48:53,680
The entire car angled from side to side on rough roads.

680
00:48:54,640 --> 00:48:56,840
The same problem occurred with the rear axle.

681
00:48:59,680 --> 00:49:02,400
Independent front suspension solved part of this problem.

682
00:49:03,280 --> 00:49:09,640
In the early 1930s, Cadillac engineer Maurice Ali experimented with the European system

683
00:49:09,641 --> 00:49:15,020
of attaching each of the front wheel assemblies to the sides of the frame instead of a long,

684
00:49:15,021 --> 00:49:17,880
solid axle.

685
00:49:17,881 --> 00:49:22,320
Arms connected a wheel to the frame, and a variety of joints, springs, and coils allowed

686
00:49:22,321 --> 00:49:26,860
the wheel to turn and absorb bumps.

687
00:49:26,861 --> 00:49:34,020
If you're going over a very rough road, instead of the axle going like this, the wheels tend

688
00:49:34,021 --> 00:49:38,640
to follow the bumps, and it makes not only for a better, smoother ride, it also makes

689
00:49:38,641 --> 00:49:41,100
the car handled better.

690
00:49:41,101 --> 00:49:45,340
Most 1934 General Motors models had this system,

691
00:49:45,341 --> 00:49:50,220
and other companies quickly jumped on the bandwagon.

692
00:49:50,221 --> 00:49:52,680
In the late 40s, engineer Earl McPherson

693
00:49:52,681 --> 00:49:55,760
moved from GM to Ford.

694
00:49:55,761 --> 00:49:58,660
He developed a system that combined a coil spring and a

695
00:49:58,661 --> 00:50:02,420
shock absorber into one unit.

696
00:50:02,421 --> 00:50:07,100
It became known as the McPherson Strut Suspension System.

697
00:50:07,101 --> 00:50:09,640
It didn't necessarily offer a smoother ride,

698
00:50:09,641 --> 00:50:13,700
but it was widely used for other reasons.

699
00:50:13,701 --> 00:50:17,300
Most of the reasons for going to the McPherson suspension

700
00:50:17,301 --> 00:50:24,600
was for the interest of simplicity, lower cost,

701
00:50:24,601 --> 00:50:27,800
and it was more compact, particularly

702
00:50:27,801 --> 00:50:30,660
when they went to the all front wheel drive,

703
00:50:30,661 --> 00:50:31,900
front engine cars.

704
00:50:31,901 --> 00:50:35,260
There wasn't much space left there.

705
00:50:35,261 --> 00:50:42,100
The rear independent suspension appeared in Europe during the 1950s, but American manufacturers

706
00:50:42,101 --> 00:50:47,160
didn't adopt it until the 1970s and 80s.

707
00:50:47,161 --> 00:50:52,440
By this time, most American cars used front-wheel drive, meaning the turning power of the engine

708
00:50:52,441 --> 00:50:56,160
went to the front wheels instead of the rear wheels.

709
00:50:56,161 --> 00:51:00,820
Since the rear wheels didn't have to turn or supply power, it was easier to remove the

710
00:51:00,821 --> 00:51:07,360
axle and provide them with independent suspension. But by the late 70s, luxury

711
00:51:07,361 --> 00:51:11,920
cars have become sophisticated enough to offer independent suspension on all

712
00:51:11,921 --> 00:51:20,980
four wheels. Another innovation known as active suspension first appeared on

713
00:51:20,981 --> 00:51:25,820
race cars then on a few high priced models in the 1980s and 90s, such as the

714
00:51:25,821 --> 00:51:27,620
and several Mercedes models.

715
00:51:30,560 --> 00:51:33,500
This complex system used sensors and hydraulics

716
00:51:33,501 --> 00:51:37,960
to actually pull a wheel up as soon as it sensed a bump,

717
00:51:37,961 --> 00:51:42,840
instead of letting the springs react to the wheel's movements.

718
00:51:42,841 --> 00:51:46,300
I drove an active suspension Corvette in the mid-'80s.

719
00:51:46,301 --> 00:51:47,880
It was truly remarkable.

720
00:51:47,881 --> 00:51:49,380
But even under mass production, there's

721
00:51:49,381 --> 00:51:50,680
just a lot of hardware there.

722
00:51:50,681 --> 00:51:53,320
And it's going to cost more than even the most sophisticated

723
00:51:53,321 --> 00:51:54,380
shocks and springs.

724
00:51:55,820 --> 00:52:01,780
As with all other parts of the car, electronics have bounced their way into today's suspension systems.

725
00:52:02,820 --> 00:52:05,920
Shock absorbers can now be set for different amounts of stiffness

726
00:52:05,921 --> 00:52:09,980
and adjust themselves up to 10 times a second during driving.

727
00:52:11,360 --> 00:52:17,620
Future suspensions will execute even faster adjustments to provide the smoothest ride at all times.

728
00:52:19,940 --> 00:52:24,380
So how do a bunch of explosions under the hood of our tourists make the wheels turn?

729
00:52:24,381 --> 00:52:30,380
while the answer is the drive train and the transmission is one of its most important parts.

730
00:52:31,580 --> 00:52:35,360
Today's automatic transmissions are filled with transmission fluid,

731
00:52:35,920 --> 00:52:38,540
which is three times thinner than the average motor oil,

732
00:52:38,880 --> 00:52:41,800
and a variety of pretty strange looking pieces.

733
00:52:42,500 --> 00:52:45,800
One of the most important parts is the torque converter.

734
00:52:46,640 --> 00:52:49,820
We'll get an inside look at the amazing way it works in a few minutes.

735
00:52:50,300 --> 00:52:53,760
First, let's try to imagine a car without a transmission.

736
00:52:54,380 --> 00:52:57,580
As soon as you turned the key, your car would speed forward.

737
00:52:58,040 --> 00:53:01,880
And the only way to stop it would be to turn the engine off and then slam on the brakes.

738
00:53:03,840 --> 00:53:10,100
Early inventors created transmissions with belts or chains or combinations of both to engage the wheels.

739
00:53:11,420 --> 00:53:13,820
Many resembled bicycle chains and gears.

740
00:53:15,160 --> 00:53:18,000
Drivers could change between low, high, and reverse.

741
00:53:18,001 --> 00:53:21,760
worse.

742
00:53:21,761 --> 00:53:28,240
Then in 1901, the American auto car model adopted the French invention of the drive shaft,

743
00:53:28,241 --> 00:53:32,540
which connected the crankshaft to the rear axle.

744
00:53:32,541 --> 00:53:35,420
That was important because you didn't have the problem of the chains.

745
00:53:35,421 --> 00:53:37,480
The chains tended to stretch.

746
00:53:37,481 --> 00:53:39,120
They tended to break.

747
00:53:39,121 --> 00:53:44,960
And once you got the concept of the shaft drive, it was a straight through drive.

748
00:53:44,961 --> 00:53:50,300
When the engine's spinning motion reached the rear axle, gears inside the differential

749
00:53:50,301 --> 00:53:56,800
transferred the sideways spinning motion into forward or reverse spinning of the wheels.

750
00:53:56,801 --> 00:54:04,540
All other American car builders quickly ditched chains and used the drive shaft.

751
00:54:04,541 --> 00:54:09,120
But there was still no rhyme or reason to how cars changed gears.

752
00:54:09,121 --> 00:54:16,880
Many of the hand levers were even located on the outside of the vehicle.

753
00:54:16,881 --> 00:54:22,160
Ford's Model T used a system which was easier than most since it had three foot pedals and

754
00:54:22,161 --> 00:54:24,080
no hand levers.

755
00:54:24,081 --> 00:54:28,460
It was known as a planetary transmission.

756
00:54:28,461 --> 00:54:34,060
The planetary transmission was one of the most significant elements of the Model T because

757
00:54:34,061 --> 00:54:36,560
it made the Model T simple to drive.

758
00:54:36,561 --> 00:54:41,940
And that was an important part of why the vehicle became so popular.

759
00:54:41,941 --> 00:54:44,440
By the 1920s, most other car makers

760
00:54:44,441 --> 00:54:47,340
adopted three-speed sliding gear systems,

761
00:54:47,341 --> 00:54:49,940
like today's manual transmissions.

762
00:54:49,941 --> 00:54:52,080
Pushing the clutch foot pedal allowed drivers

763
00:54:52,081 --> 00:54:55,740
to briefly disengage the gears and move a hand lever

764
00:54:55,741 --> 00:54:59,280
into the desired gear.

765
00:54:59,281 --> 00:55:03,560
Four-speed transmissions became popular in the 1930s.

766
00:55:03,561 --> 00:55:08,200
Some drivers had trouble steering while shifting this many gears.

767
00:55:08,201 --> 00:55:16,160
Although people who drove this badly usually had a few drinks in them too.

768
00:55:16,161 --> 00:55:22,060
Driving became a lot easier in 1939 when Oldsmobile introduced its Hydromatic transmission, their

769
00:55:22,061 --> 00:55:24,320
brand name for an automatic transmission.

770
00:55:24,321 --> 00:55:26,120
...direction selector.

771
00:55:26,121 --> 00:55:31,080
Just set the selector at drive, then step on the accelerator and you're off.

772
00:55:31,081 --> 00:55:33,420
All shifting is automatic.

773
00:55:33,421 --> 00:55:38,240
Other car companies rushed to market with their own automatics until World War II halted

774
00:55:38,241 --> 00:55:40,260
civilian car production.

775
00:55:40,261 --> 00:55:44,240
But the war also had some unexpected benefits for transmissions.

776
00:55:44,241 --> 00:55:50,240
Hydramatic automatic transmissions were used in U.S. produced tanks.

777
00:55:50,241 --> 00:55:55,140
And in order to become durable enough and reliable enough to be used in tanks, a tremendous

778
00:55:55,141 --> 00:55:58,300
amount of additional development work was done.

779
00:55:58,301 --> 00:56:05,400
Thanks to tanks, post-war drivers enjoyed better automatics.

780
00:56:05,401 --> 00:56:09,700
The key to an automatic transmission is the torque converter.

781
00:56:09,701 --> 00:56:14,620
In a manual transmission, metal gears directly connect the engine to the drive shaft.

782
00:56:14,621 --> 00:56:20,160
But in a torque converter, the engine spins a fan-like object encased in transmission

783
00:56:20,161 --> 00:56:22,300
fluid.

784
00:56:22,301 --> 00:56:26,740
When the brake pedal is released, another fan across the fluid-filled chamber is allowed

785
00:56:26,741 --> 00:56:28,240
to move.

786
00:56:28,241 --> 00:56:33,920
The second fan eventually connects the drive shaft and wheels.

787
00:56:33,921 --> 00:56:38,960
The spinning motion from the engine fan is transmitted through the fluid and causes the

788
00:56:38,961 --> 00:56:44,620
second fan to gradually increase its speed until it matches the engine fan.

789
00:56:44,621 --> 00:56:50,200
It has a big advantage in that it cushions changes.

790
00:56:50,201 --> 00:56:53,240
Take your foot off the brake and now you step on the gas pedal.

791
00:56:53,241 --> 00:56:56,000
Well, you don't jerk that hard in the vehicle

792
00:56:56,001 --> 00:56:58,600
because it gradually starts increasing the speed

793
00:56:58,601 --> 00:57:01,320
going into the transmission, which connects to the wheels.

794
00:57:02,720 --> 00:57:05,620
Since you didn't have to use a clutch or shift gears,

795
00:57:06,400 --> 00:57:09,760
the ease and simplicity of automatic transmissions

796
00:57:09,761 --> 00:57:12,080
helped open the car market to more people

797
00:57:12,081 --> 00:57:14,360
than almost any other automotive invention.

798
00:57:17,720 --> 00:57:21,200
Another old idea that found new life decades later

799
00:57:21,201 --> 00:57:22,900
was front-wheel drive.

800
00:57:23,240 --> 00:57:28,240
The stylish Cordell 29 had front-wheel drive in 1929.

801
00:57:28,241 --> 00:57:35,240
Forty years later, as oil prices rose, car makers were building cars for better fuel economy.

802
00:57:35,241 --> 00:57:44,240
Engineers squeezed more space out of smaller vehicles by turning the engine sideways and using front-wheel drive.

803
00:57:44,241 --> 00:57:50,240
This meant the turning power was going to the front wheels instead of through a drive shaft to the rear wheels.

804
00:57:50,241 --> 00:57:53,080
wheels.

805
00:57:53,081 --> 00:57:57,160
Many people felt that front wheel drive also provided better traction.

806
00:57:57,161 --> 00:58:04,280
But recently, some companies have changed back to rear wheel drive.

807
00:58:04,281 --> 00:58:08,580
The problem is that ultimately when it comes to handling and performance, front wheel drive

808
00:58:08,581 --> 00:58:13,240
has its limitations because you're basically asking the front wheels to do too much.

809
00:58:13,241 --> 00:58:17,160
They have to support a bunch of the weight of the car, they have to help with cornering,

810
00:58:17,161 --> 00:58:23,760
They have to steer and they also have to put the power down.

811
00:58:23,761 --> 00:58:30,400
Future transmissions, whether front or rear wheel drive, will become even more efficient.

812
00:58:30,401 --> 00:58:34,060
Six speed cars are already starting to appear.

813
00:58:34,061 --> 00:58:39,460
The more speeds or gear ratios a driver has to choose from means the engine and the transmission

814
00:58:39,461 --> 00:58:43,400
can work together more efficiently.

815
00:58:43,401 --> 00:58:47,400
Imagine if you could have an unlimited amount of speeds to choose from.

816
00:58:47,401 --> 00:58:50,400
Well, automotive engineers already have.

817
00:58:50,401 --> 00:58:55,400
They're now developing continuously variable transmissions.

818
00:58:55,401 --> 00:59:00,400
Electronics will allow the transmission to smoothly move from one speed to the next

819
00:59:00,401 --> 00:59:07,400
in an unlimited number of ratios and increase gas mileage by up to 20%.

820
00:59:07,401 --> 00:59:11,400
It seems that everything in the car is getting better.

821
00:59:11,401 --> 00:59:16,400
Even the dependable old steering wheel is going through some major changes.

822
00:59:16,401 --> 00:59:20,400
We'll swerve into steering next.

823
00:59:20,401 --> 00:59:28,400
Before 1920, a squeaky clean inventor attached a watertight box to the side of his car.

824
00:59:28,401 --> 00:59:33,400
Soap and water cleaned his clothes as the car bounced along.

825
00:59:33,401 --> 00:59:38,400
The Body Shop will continue on Modern Marvels.

826
00:59:41,400 --> 00:59:45,400
Now, the Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

827
00:59:45,401 --> 00:59:49,400
Well, finally, something we can wrap our hands around

828
00:59:49,401 --> 00:59:53,400
without getting them greasy. You use the steering wheel every time you

829
00:59:53,401 --> 00:59:57,400
drive, but do you really know how it works? Well, we're going to show you.

830
00:59:57,401 --> 01:00:01,400
Chris, take it out.

831
01:00:11,400 --> 01:00:18,860
Besides the wheel, there are three parts.

832
01:00:19,200 --> 01:00:24,960
The steering column, the steering gears, which are located right in here, and the tie rods.

833
01:00:24,961 --> 01:00:32,580
Now, when you turn the steering wheel, these gears push or pull the tie rod in one direction or another, which turns the wheels.

834
01:00:32,581 --> 01:00:41,580
And like we saw with the brakes, the brute force required to operate this part of a car in the early days of driving became a breeze later on.

835
01:00:41,581 --> 01:00:46,580
Before the turn of the century, cars didn't even have steering wheels.

836
01:00:46,581 --> 01:00:53,580
Drivers used simple sticks known as tillers which kept them on their toes.

837
01:00:53,581 --> 01:01:00,580
Decades later, Henry Ford still enjoyed the challenge of maneuvering his first vehicle from 1896.

838
01:01:00,581 --> 01:01:06,380
Small movements caused the car to constantly change direction.

839
01:01:06,980 --> 01:01:11,320
And shock from bumps in the road made the tiller want to jump out of a driver's hand.

840
01:01:13,840 --> 01:01:19,480
Driving a vehicle with a tiller, when you had those old dirt roads, it requires some considerable effort.

841
01:01:19,760 --> 01:01:24,520
And even on modern roads, you have to hold on to that tiller every single minute.

842
01:01:24,980 --> 01:01:25,700
It was alarming.

843
01:01:30,580 --> 01:01:38,460
The steering wheel sent the tiller packing just after the turn of the century.

844
01:01:38,461 --> 01:01:43,380
It was easier for drivers to hold onto, and the steering column absorbed shock or feedback

845
01:01:43,381 --> 01:01:45,840
from the road.

846
01:01:45,841 --> 01:01:49,880
Less feedback meant that the wheel didn't try to twist its way out of a driver's hand

847
01:01:49,881 --> 01:01:54,100
like the tillers did.

848
01:01:54,101 --> 01:01:59,940
Early steering columns usually used a worm gear system, also known as recirculating ball

849
01:01:59,941 --> 01:02:05,460
steering to turn the wheels. At the end of the column were threads just like the

850
01:02:05,461 --> 01:02:11,400
threads on a screw. As the driver turned the steering wheel the threads caused

851
01:02:11,401 --> 01:02:16,540
the other part of the gear to twist to the left or right. The arm attached at

852
01:02:16,541 --> 01:02:20,760
the end of this part of the gear then pushed the tie rods connected to both

853
01:02:20,761 --> 01:02:25,100
front wheels in the appropriate direction.

854
01:02:25,101 --> 01:02:32,700
A few cars in the early 1900s began experimenting with rack and pinion steering, like the first

855
01:02:32,701 --> 01:02:35,800
Cadillac in 1903.

856
01:02:35,801 --> 01:02:40,880
The rack part of the gear was just that, a long rack of gear teeth.

857
01:02:40,881 --> 01:02:46,360
The pinion piece was a small round nub with similar teeth.

858
01:02:46,361 --> 01:02:51,980
As the driver turned the wheel, the pinion rotated, causing the rack to move.

859
01:02:51,981 --> 01:02:56,420
the rack pushed the wheels in the same direction.

860
01:02:56,421 --> 01:02:59,520
But the worm won out, since it was easier to turn

861
01:02:59,521 --> 01:03:03,280
and absorbed more feedback.

862
01:03:03,281 --> 01:03:06,700
However, as car makers produced larger and larger models

863
01:03:06,701 --> 01:03:11,540
after World War II, steering became difficult.

864
01:03:11,541 --> 01:03:14,780
Then in 1951, Chrysler and Buick

865
01:03:14,781 --> 01:03:17,860
introduced similar power steering systems.

866
01:03:17,861 --> 01:03:20,760
Just as hydraulics had improved braking,

867
01:03:20,761 --> 01:03:25,260
they put the power into power steering.

868
01:03:25,261 --> 01:03:27,400
When the driver turned the wheel, hydraulics

869
01:03:27,401 --> 01:03:29,460
pushed the steering gear in the right direction.

870
01:03:34,660 --> 01:03:38,060
This hydraulic helping hand made it easier for drivers

871
01:03:38,061 --> 01:03:40,100
to turn the wheel.

872
01:03:40,101 --> 01:03:41,860
Power steering allowed many people

873
01:03:41,861 --> 01:03:45,320
to really enjoy driving for the first time.

874
01:03:45,321 --> 01:03:48,380
But in some ways, engineers in the 50s

875
01:03:48,381 --> 01:03:51,040
may have made steering too easy.

876
01:03:51,041 --> 01:03:53,920
It was like the steering wheel was mounted in butter.

877
01:03:53,921 --> 01:03:56,520
And you could spin these things with one finger,

878
01:03:56,521 --> 01:03:58,380
but there was no road feedback.

879
01:03:58,381 --> 01:03:59,980
You knew the car went in this direction,

880
01:03:59,981 --> 01:04:03,640
so the wheel must be connected to the steering wheel,

881
01:04:03,641 --> 01:04:06,560
but it sure didn't feel like it.

882
01:04:06,561 --> 01:04:10,320
In the 1970s, manufacturers developed better systems

883
01:04:10,321 --> 01:04:13,240
that made steering easier, but still gave drivers

884
01:04:13,241 --> 01:04:16,180
a feel for the road.

885
01:04:16,181 --> 01:04:21,680
By this time, the harder to turn rack and pinion system made a comeback in light cars.

886
01:04:21,681 --> 01:04:26,120
Race cars had been using its more precise handling features for several decades.

887
01:04:26,121 --> 01:04:32,980
It's a very small package, so it goes well into a small vehicle.

888
01:04:32,981 --> 01:04:42,340
The smaller vehicles weigh less, so the disadvantages of rack and pinion are not as noticeable.

889
01:04:42,341 --> 01:04:48,580
the heavier vehicle rack and pinion can be a problem.

890
01:04:48,581 --> 01:04:51,780
In the last 20 years, engineers combined rack and pinion's

891
01:04:51,781 --> 01:04:55,200
accurate handling with hydraulics' ease of turning.

892
01:04:55,201 --> 01:04:58,120
This has helped rack and pinion spread beyond the economy

893
01:04:58,121 --> 01:05:01,940
market into larger cars.

894
01:05:01,941 --> 01:05:05,540
Then in the late 1990s, engineers married steering

895
01:05:05,541 --> 01:05:07,580
and electronics.

896
01:05:07,581 --> 01:05:10,540
Microprocessors connected to the speedometer

897
01:05:10,541 --> 01:05:14,380
could adjust the steering of the car at different speeds.

898
01:05:14,381 --> 01:05:16,720
This was called variable ratio steering.

899
01:05:19,440 --> 01:05:24,520
At low speeds, the system makes the wheel easy to turn rapidly.

900
01:05:24,521 --> 01:05:26,520
On the highway, the steering is stiffer

901
01:05:26,521 --> 01:05:31,340
to prevent motorists from oversteering or swerving.

902
01:05:31,341 --> 01:05:33,960
And just like modern brakes, steering

903
01:05:33,961 --> 01:05:37,240
systems are on the verge of moving beyond hydraulics

904
01:05:37,241 --> 01:05:39,400
to electric systems.

905
01:05:39,401 --> 01:05:44,300
electric power steering will be available in the next few years.

906
01:05:44,301 --> 01:05:46,440
As soon as the wheel turns, a miniature motor

907
01:05:46,441 --> 01:05:48,800
at the end of the steering column will activate

908
01:05:48,801 --> 01:05:51,840
and move the tie rods.

909
01:05:51,841 --> 01:05:56,120
It has a dramatic increase in fuel economy.

910
01:05:56,121 --> 01:05:59,820
It feels even better than a hydraulic power steering system.

911
01:05:59,821 --> 01:06:02,960
And it basically will replace the hydraulic power steering

912
01:06:02,961 --> 01:06:05,060
systems because it's more efficient.

913
01:06:05,061 --> 01:06:13,360
The fully electronic systems of the future, known as steer by wire, will completely eliminate

914
01:06:13,361 --> 01:06:15,120
gears.

915
01:06:15,121 --> 01:06:21,060
Steering wheels will simply be a way for drivers to feed electronic signals to the wheels.

916
01:06:21,061 --> 01:06:28,240
Drivers may even steer with a joystick.

917
01:06:28,241 --> 01:06:33,740
Now we've just seen how electricity helps with the steering, but it does a lot more.

918
01:06:33,741 --> 01:06:37,340
This 12-volt battery is the key to the car's electrical system.

919
01:06:37,341 --> 01:06:41,840
It powers the lights and all the accessories when the engine is off, and energizes the

920
01:06:41,841 --> 01:06:44,680
starting motor when you turn the key.

921
01:06:44,681 --> 01:06:49,500
This motor spins for a short time in order to get the engine running, which in turn powers

922
01:06:49,501 --> 01:06:51,660
the alternator.

923
01:06:51,661 --> 01:06:56,980
The alternator is a little powerhouse that sends current to all the electrical components

924
01:06:56,981 --> 01:06:58,660
throughout the car.

925
01:06:58,661 --> 01:07:00,760
It even recharges the battery.

926
01:07:00,761 --> 01:07:02,260
Edison would have been proud.

927
01:07:03,740 --> 01:07:10,740
Electricity has been a vital component of automobiles since the very first models.

928
01:07:10,741 --> 01:07:18,740
Without that tiny spark at the end of the spark plug, the engine would just be a heavy piece of modern art.

929
01:07:18,741 --> 01:07:24,740
In the first few decades of driving, motorists had to use a hand crank to start the engine.

930
01:07:24,741 --> 01:07:31,740
Turning the crank moved the cylinders and drew energy from 6-volt batteries.

931
01:07:31,741 --> 01:07:35,200
Once the crank had been turned a few times, the engine would catch.

932
01:07:35,980 --> 01:07:40,940
This meant that it started to provide enough power by itself to draw electricity from the batteries.

933
01:07:42,560 --> 01:07:48,720
But crank starting a car was strenuous, and engines didn't always catch the first few times.

934
01:07:49,680 --> 01:07:53,740
This was the number one thing that would injure you in automobile operation.

935
01:07:54,260 --> 01:07:57,460
The term cranky comes from having a bad day crank starting your automobile.

936
01:07:57,461 --> 01:08:02,460
If the engine misfired, the crank could quickly and powerfully

937
01:08:02,560 --> 01:08:06,200
kick backwards in the motorist's hands.

938
01:08:06,300 --> 01:08:09,700
Broken fingers or arms were common.

939
01:08:09,800 --> 01:08:11,900
Since the crank was low on the car,

940
01:08:12,000 --> 01:08:13,700
the person's head would also be low,

941
01:08:13,800 --> 01:08:17,720
and sometimes even more serious injuries occurred.

942
01:08:17,820 --> 01:08:21,560
This was the case in 1910 when a friend of Cadillac's Henry

943
01:08:21,660 --> 01:08:24,760
Leland was critically hurt.

944
01:08:24,761 --> 01:08:30,860
It came back up, struck him in the side of the head, and he ended up dying from his injuries

945
01:08:30,861 --> 01:08:33,100
in the hospital at a later date.

946
01:08:33,101 --> 01:08:39,720
This so upset Henry Leland that he set his engineers to a task to eliminate the crank.

947
01:08:39,721 --> 01:08:42,960
Then Leland hired a man named Charles Kettering.

948
01:08:42,961 --> 01:08:48,520
Kettering had already developed a small, powerful electric motor for use inside cash registers.

949
01:08:48,521 --> 01:08:58,240
By 1911, he used a similar type motor to provide crank-free electric starting for cars.

950
01:08:58,241 --> 01:09:02,620
The driver pushed a button to activate the electric motor.

951
01:09:02,621 --> 01:09:07,420
A belt on this motor turned a shaft attached to the gas engine for a few seconds until

952
01:09:07,421 --> 01:09:12,920
the engine was able to power itself.

953
01:09:12,921 --> 01:09:19,080
Henry Leland quickly installed electric starters on all 1912 Cadillacs.

954
01:09:19,081 --> 01:09:24,540
Just three years later, virtually every American car used this type of starter.

955
01:09:24,541 --> 01:09:26,040
It was unanimous.

956
01:09:26,041 --> 01:09:28,640
The crank stank.

957
01:09:28,641 --> 01:09:36,800
So consequently, the automobile became available to more people, the elderly.

958
01:09:36,801 --> 01:09:40,080
Became available to the infirm.

959
01:09:40,081 --> 01:09:42,120
Became available to women.

960
01:09:42,121 --> 01:09:49,120
Self-starter was certainly one of those inventions that had a tremendous impact on the desirability of the automobile.

961
01:09:54,720 --> 01:09:58,220
But Kettering's system did even more.

962
01:09:58,221 --> 01:10:03,620
Once the gas engine was running, it was able to send power back to the electric motor.

963
01:10:03,621 --> 01:10:08,620
At this point, the motor became a generator, which charged the battery on the side of the car.

964
01:10:08,621 --> 01:10:16,840
Kettering used this power to run electric headlights for the first time on the same 1912

965
01:10:16,841 --> 01:10:23,560
Cadillac. Early cars had mainly used oil lanterns, which did not provide much light.

966
01:10:24,760 --> 01:10:28,400
They could even get blown out if wind got inside the glass casing.

967
01:10:29,980 --> 01:10:34,195
Electric lights changed the way you drove a car at night.

968
01:10:34,196 --> 01:10:36,340
The lights prior to that were

969
01:10:36,341 --> 01:10:37,840
basically to see the car.

970
01:10:37,841 --> 01:10:39,640
But now with electric lights, you're actually

971
01:10:39,641 --> 01:10:42,980
being able to cast out enough beam that allows you to see

972
01:10:42,981 --> 01:10:43,980
where you're actually heading.

973
01:10:49,180 --> 01:10:52,620
Then in the mid-1950s, as the use of electric equipment

974
01:10:52,621 --> 01:10:56,100
like interior lights, electric horns, and radios increased,

975
01:10:56,101 --> 01:11:01,140
manufacturers switched from 6-volt batteries to 12 volts.

976
01:11:01,141 --> 01:11:02,900
Higher voltage allowed the battery

977
01:11:02,901 --> 01:11:06,340
to keep up with the growing demand for electric devices.

978
01:11:06,341 --> 01:11:11,200
For instance, Packard had already introduced power windows in 1941.

979
01:11:16,920 --> 01:11:21,600
In 1960, the car's electrical system became even more sophisticated

980
01:11:21,601 --> 01:11:25,840
when Chrysler introduced the alternator on its Valiant model.

981
01:11:26,900 --> 01:11:30,360
The alternator was simply a more efficient generator.

982
01:11:30,361 --> 01:11:35,360
When electronics entered the automobile in the 1970s,

983
01:11:35,460 --> 01:11:38,960
the amount of electrical power needed rose once again.

984
01:11:39,060 --> 01:11:40,900
Today, as microprocessors continue

985
01:11:41,000 --> 01:11:44,000
to make virtually every part of the car more efficient,

986
01:11:44,100 --> 01:11:49,220
a 12-volt battery is on the verge of becoming overburdened.

987
01:11:52,720 --> 01:11:56,560
A car maybe in the 50s may have had like 50 circuits,

988
01:11:56,660 --> 01:11:57,960
wiring circuits.

989
01:11:57,961 --> 01:12:01,500
circuits. Today there's about 1,800 of them.

990
01:12:01,501 --> 01:12:04,960
So I mean, the car has become very complex.

991
01:12:04,961 --> 01:12:14,960
Researchers are now exploring the possibility of boosting the car battery from 12 volts to 42 volts in the next 5 to 10 years.

992
01:12:14,961 --> 01:12:22,960
Higher voltage will allow future batteries to run all of today's and tomorrow's amazing electronic systems.

993
01:12:27,960 --> 01:12:31,560
A single spark plug fires about 15 times every second,

994
01:12:31,561 --> 01:12:35,000
or one million times every thousand miles.

995
01:12:35,001 --> 01:12:38,440
The Body Shop will continue on Modern Marvels.

996
01:12:43,640 --> 01:12:47,240
We now return to The Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

997
01:12:47,241 --> 01:13:00,780
With all the time we spend in the car these days, it's practically a second home.

998
01:13:00,781 --> 01:13:03,960
And we want it as comfortable as our first home.

999
01:13:03,961 --> 01:13:10,760
Multi-speaker sound systems, built-in phones, plush seats, lumbar supports, all designed

1000
01:13:10,761 --> 01:13:14,540
to transport us in luxury and keep us connected.

1001
01:13:14,541 --> 01:13:18,540
adding creature comforts to cars, well, that's nothing new.

1002
01:13:22,220 --> 01:13:26,820
The earliest car seats were slightly padded, non-adjustable wooden benches.

1003
01:13:27,620 --> 01:13:31,800
If your legs weren't long enough to reach the pedals, you were simply out of luck.

1004
01:13:33,780 --> 01:13:39,700
Then in 1914, Maxwell became the first company to offer an adjustable front seat.

1005
01:13:39,701 --> 01:13:48,300
As interiors grew fancier, keeping folks out of cars became important.

1006
01:13:48,301 --> 01:13:51,080
The first door locks appeared in the early 1920s.

1007
01:13:51,081 --> 01:13:57,380
They were vital because there was little else to stop early car thieves.

1008
01:13:57,381 --> 01:14:00,280
Prior to this, vehicles did not have locks.

1009
01:14:00,281 --> 01:14:02,500
There were no ignition locks.

1010
01:14:02,501 --> 01:14:04,300
There were no door locks, nothing.

1011
01:14:04,301 --> 01:14:09,400
And in fact, someone could get into your car, start it up and drive away.

1012
01:14:09,401 --> 01:14:11,400
if you left it parked on the street.

1013
01:14:13,400 --> 01:14:15,640
Keys were not used to unlock the steering column

1014
01:14:15,641 --> 01:14:19,640
and start the car until Chrysler introduced this in 1949.

1015
01:14:21,240 --> 01:14:25,000
Before that, drivers started cars in a wide variety of ways,

1016
01:14:25,001 --> 01:14:27,640
including buttons, foot pedals, or switches.

1017
01:14:30,000 --> 01:14:32,640
Another reason for owners to lock their doors

1018
01:14:32,641 --> 01:14:36,000
was the development of car radios in the late 20s.

1019
01:14:36,001 --> 01:14:39,140
Typically, in those days, it would take two trained

1020
01:14:39,240 --> 01:14:41,740
technicians two to three days to install a car radio

1021
01:14:41,840 --> 01:14:42,940
in your vehicle.

1022
01:14:43,040 --> 01:14:48,040
They would weigh over 20 pounds the size of a car battery.

1023
01:14:48,140 --> 01:14:50,740
Not only were radios big, their price

1024
01:14:50,840 --> 01:14:52,660
tags were enormous.

1025
01:14:52,760 --> 01:14:56,560
At a time when consumers could buy a car for about $600,

1026
01:14:56,660 --> 01:15:02,860
the cost of a radio and installation was almost $400.

1027
01:15:02,960 --> 01:15:05,360
Now, things sure are a lot better today.

1028
01:15:05,361 --> 01:15:11,140
And in the future, drivers will be able to receive a wide range of digital stations by satellite.

1029
01:15:12,760 --> 01:15:20,200
And that's basically going to be like a cable TV for your car radio, where you'll have your choice of up to 100 channels of different information.

1030
01:15:20,201 --> 01:15:25,200
It's going to give you more and more choices and a wider range of material to listen to in the car.

1031
01:15:28,940 --> 01:15:34,460
But drivers and passengers would have a very hard time enjoying all of these conveniences inside their cars.

1032
01:15:34,461 --> 01:15:36,960
if they were sweating or freezing.

1033
01:15:38,340 --> 01:15:41,460
Heating and air conditioning are two of the most basic

1034
01:15:41,461 --> 01:15:43,160
and important creature comforts.

1035
01:15:44,020 --> 01:15:46,860
Yet these systems took quite some time to develop.

1036
01:15:48,340 --> 01:15:50,960
Early drivers wore elaborate storm aprons,

1037
01:15:51,140 --> 01:15:53,760
giving new meaning to one size fits all.

1038
01:15:54,580 --> 01:15:57,260
These aprons and heavy coats were the primary means

1039
01:15:57,261 --> 01:15:59,620
of protection against cold, wet conditions.

1040
01:15:59,621 --> 01:16:05,620
As closed cars entered the market in the 1920s,

1041
01:16:05,621 --> 01:16:09,620
more elaborate systems could be developed.

1042
01:16:09,621 --> 01:16:13,620
Since the engine generated a lot of heat, some well-meaning

1043
01:16:13,621 --> 01:16:17,620
but very misguided inventors thought that hot exhaust fumes

1044
01:16:17,621 --> 01:16:20,620
would be a good source of warmth.

1045
01:16:20,621 --> 01:16:24,620
Exhaust gas is highly toxic, and fortunately,

1046
01:16:24,621 --> 01:16:27,620
those early automobiles were not leak-proof,

1047
01:16:27,621 --> 01:16:32,080
leak-proof, but there used to be fires in the passenger compartment.

1048
01:16:32,081 --> 01:16:38,900
Sometimes it would burn your soles when you put your feet over the exhaust gas foot warmer.

1049
01:16:38,901 --> 01:16:45,280
It was clear that a safer form of heating needed to be developed.

1050
01:16:45,281 --> 01:16:51,260
In 1926, semi-portable hot water car heaters became available.

1051
01:16:51,261 --> 01:16:55,580
Water heated in the engine compartment passed through a maze of small tubes just behind a

1052
01:16:55,581 --> 01:16:56,281
fan.

1053
01:16:56,282 --> 01:17:04,820
Then in 1937, Nash introduced a built-in heating system that drew fresh outside air

1054
01:17:04,821 --> 01:17:11,980
into the engine compartment, where it was filtered, heated, and released into the interior.

1055
01:17:11,981 --> 01:17:19,160
Nash's system became the model for all other heaters.

1056
01:17:19,161 --> 01:17:25,180
Keeping cool in hot weather was seen as more of a luxury, and therefore developed later.

1057
01:17:25,181 --> 01:17:27,202
In 1930, John Hammond, Jr.

1058
01:17:27,203 --> 01:17:32,180
of Houston, Texas, paid the Kelvinator Refrigerator Company over

1059
01:17:32,181 --> 01:17:37,780
$1,000 to mount a special cooling unit on the back of his Cadillac.

1060
01:17:37,781 --> 01:17:44,660
This bulky experiment became the first air conditioning system in a car.

1061
01:17:44,661 --> 01:17:50,080
By 1939, engineers at Packard spread coils and condensers and compressors throughout

1062
01:17:50,081 --> 01:17:55,440
the car to create the first commercial air conditioning system.

1063
01:17:55,441 --> 01:18:01,900
In 1954, the Harrison Radiator Division of GM finally produced an air conditioning system

1064
01:18:01,901 --> 01:18:05,040
small enough to fit entirely under the hood.

1065
01:18:05,041 --> 01:18:10,780
It first appeared in the 1954 Pontiac.

1066
01:18:10,781 --> 01:18:14,360
Over the years, heaters and air conditioners have been combined.

1067
01:18:14,361 --> 01:18:19,180
Electronics have also allowed drivers to set specific temperatures inside the cabin.

1068
01:18:20,040 --> 01:18:26,540
And in the next few years, motorists won't have to set any buttons at all, thanks to so-called smart sensors.

1069
01:18:27,460 --> 01:18:35,380
We envision them being introduced in the air conditioning system to sense the skin temperature,

1070
01:18:35,381 --> 01:18:40,380
temperature, the moisture level at the skin, and also voice

1071
01:18:40,480 --> 01:18:45,480
recognition system to provide individualized control of the

1072
01:18:45,580 --> 01:18:47,480
temperature.

1073
01:18:51,580 --> 01:18:55,480
Future drivers will ride in climate controlled luxury.

1074
01:18:55,580 --> 01:18:58,480
But if you don't have your health, you don't have anything.

1075
01:19:05,380 --> 01:19:10,060
Gee, I hate when that happens.

1076
01:19:10,061 --> 01:19:13,280
Almost as much as when this happens.

1077
01:19:21,960 --> 01:19:24,980
Now, chances are you're never going to be hit by a sledgehammer.

1078
01:19:25,300 --> 01:19:28,920
But a serious accident could cause this same type of damage.

1079
01:19:29,660 --> 01:19:32,340
Luckily, a layer of plastic inside the glass

1080
01:19:32,341 --> 01:19:34,800
keeps it from breaking into big, dangerous pieces.

1081
01:19:35,380 --> 01:19:41,060
Now, we all like luxury, but safety is really a car's most important feature.

1082
01:19:43,120 --> 01:19:46,640
Although early driving was thrilling, it was also dangerous.

1083
01:19:47,940 --> 01:19:52,400
Before World War I, cars had killed 36,000 people in America.

1084
01:19:53,380 --> 01:19:54,080
U.S.

1085
01:19:54,081 --> 01:19:59,980
deaths in the Revolutionary War, the War of 1812, and the Spanish-American War totaled only 22,000.

1086
01:19:59,981 --> 01:20:09,360
Since early drivers sat high on top of their cars without doors, finding a way to remain

1087
01:20:09,361 --> 01:20:14,100
in the vehicle became one of the first safety goals.

1088
01:20:14,101 --> 01:20:21,160
Seat belts actually occurred as early as 1903 when there was a race, one of these high-speed

1089
01:20:21,161 --> 01:20:26,920
races, and the driver strapped himself in with a belt.

1090
01:20:26,921 --> 01:20:32,180
And that was the first use of somebody strapping themselves into an automobile.

1091
01:20:32,181 --> 01:20:38,860
But seat belts weren't widely used on consumer models for another 50 years.

1092
01:20:38,861 --> 01:20:42,660
Since early drivers weren't too concerned about buckling up, having a little cushion

1093
01:20:42,661 --> 01:20:45,960
during collisions was important.

1094
01:20:45,961 --> 01:20:52,620
Front bumpers became available as an extra option in 1906.

1095
01:20:52,621 --> 01:20:56,820
Another safety device which filtered down to the average motorist from racing was the

1096
01:20:56,821 --> 01:20:59,020
rear view mirror.

1097
01:20:59,021 --> 01:21:08,200
In 1911, Ray Haroon rigged a mirror on his car and went on to win the Indianapolis 500.

1098
01:21:08,201 --> 01:21:15,300
By the 1930s, these mirrors became more popular on consumer models.

1099
01:21:15,301 --> 01:21:21,460
Closed bodied cars in the 1920s offered motorists more structural strength, but they also raised

1100
01:21:21,461 --> 01:21:24,560
new concerns about glass.

1101
01:21:24,561 --> 01:21:26,700
Plate glass in the early models usually

1102
01:21:26,701 --> 01:21:30,280
broke into long, dangerous shards during accidents

1103
01:21:30,281 --> 01:21:34,800
or when attacked by angry baseball pitchers.

1104
01:21:34,801 --> 01:21:36,680
It was kind of a scary situation.

1105
01:21:36,681 --> 01:21:39,160
And in fact, many people in the 1920s

1106
01:21:39,161 --> 01:21:42,460
refused to ride in closed cars because of the perceived

1107
01:21:42,461 --> 01:21:46,660
danger of the shattering plate glass.

1108
01:21:46,661 --> 01:21:51,380
The invention of safety glass eased these fears.

1109
01:21:51,381 --> 01:21:56,320
In much the same way as today, manufacturers sandwiched a layer of plastic between two

1110
01:21:56,321 --> 01:21:59,560
sheets of glass.

1111
01:21:59,561 --> 01:22:04,980
When it broke, the glass either stuck to the plastic or broke off in small, round, harmless

1112
01:22:04,981 --> 01:22:08,020
pieces.

1113
01:22:08,021 --> 01:22:14,680
The 1928 Ford Model A and 28 Cadillacs were some of the very first models to use safety

1114
01:22:14,681 --> 01:22:17,000
glass.

1115
01:22:17,001 --> 01:22:21,940
Since windshields were commonplace, windshield wipers had to keep them clean to ensure good

1116
01:22:21,941 --> 01:22:23,900
visibility.

1117
01:22:23,901 --> 01:22:29,260
In 1916, drivers had to move the windshield wipers back and forth by hand.

1118
01:22:29,261 --> 01:22:34,280
A small knob on the inside of the window was attached to the blade on the outside.

1119
01:22:34,281 --> 01:22:38,620
It was clear that a better system was needed.

1120
01:22:38,621 --> 01:22:44,040
Very quickly, some clever engineers discovered that the windshield wiper could be powered

1121
01:22:44,041 --> 01:22:46,260
by a vacuum motor.

1122
01:22:46,261 --> 01:22:50,200
This was driven from the vacuum developed inside the engine.

1123
01:22:50,201 --> 01:22:53,300
The only problem was when you're driving up a hill

1124
01:22:53,301 --> 01:22:56,100
and you're pushing the engine, the vacuum in the engine

1125
01:22:56,101 --> 01:22:57,600
drops very low.

1126
01:22:57,601 --> 01:23:00,740
So you would see vehicles climbing hills in the rain,

1127
01:23:00,741 --> 01:23:02,880
and their wipers would stop operating.

1128
01:23:06,440 --> 01:23:08,880
By the 1950s, electric windshield wipers

1129
01:23:08,881 --> 01:23:11,660
were fairly common, and the older vacuum problem

1130
01:23:11,661 --> 01:23:12,460
disappeared.

1131
01:23:12,461 --> 01:23:19,460
Safety belts made a comeback, beginning with the 1949 Nash.

1132
01:23:19,461 --> 01:23:25,460
They gradually appeared on more cars in the 50s and 60s.

1133
01:23:25,461 --> 01:23:31,460
Airbags were combined with seat belts to provide even greater safety in the early 1970s.

1134
01:23:31,461 --> 01:23:38,460
But before they came on the market, researchers struggled to make these complex devices work.

1135
01:23:38,461 --> 01:23:43,460
Our first prototype of that was developed in 1971.

1136
01:23:43,560 --> 01:23:45,960
There were a lot of technical challenges,

1137
01:23:46,060 --> 01:23:48,100
because you're asking some mechanism

1138
01:23:48,200 --> 01:23:52,800
to fill a bag in a little less than 50 milliseconds,

1139
01:23:52,900 --> 01:23:56,720
which is to give you an idea of 50 milliseconds,

1140
01:23:56,820 --> 01:23:59,720
you blink your eye, and it takes 250 milliseconds.

1141
01:24:02,760 --> 01:24:04,820
When the vehicle received an impact,

1142
01:24:04,920 --> 01:24:07,520
an electronic signal raced to the airbag.

1143
01:24:07,521 --> 01:24:08,221
airbag.

1144
01:24:08,222 --> 01:24:14,520
A small charge propelled the bag beyond its housing and a pressurized gas inflator

1145
01:24:14,521 --> 01:24:16,795
quickly filled the bag.

1146
01:24:16,796 --> 01:24:23,520
The system worked well, but it was an $800 option when GM introduced

1147
01:24:24,040 --> 01:24:30,299
it in 1973. Since these cars only cost about $6,000, airbag sales fell flat.

1148
01:24:30,300 --> 01:24:31,040
They were

1149
01:24:31,041 --> 01:24:35,334
flat. They were discontinued three years later.

1150
01:24:35,335 --> 01:24:39,080
They re-emerged in 1987 due to government

1151
01:24:39,081 --> 01:24:45,695
mandates. They were less expensive and this time well received by the public.

1152
01:24:45,696 --> 01:24:46,640
Since then,

1153
01:24:46,641 --> 01:24:51,326
airbags have started to pop up in many areas around the interior.

1154
01:24:51,327 --> 01:24:52,840
Passenger airbags and

1155
01:24:52,841 --> 01:24:55,891
side impact systems are now fairly common.

1156
01:24:55,892 --> 01:24:59,160
The newest type is known as a curtain airbag,

1157
01:24:59,161 --> 01:25:03,360
which expands from the roof, protects the driver if the car rolls over.

1158
01:25:07,000 --> 01:25:10,340
But there's still a need for airbags to become smarter.

1159
01:25:10,980 --> 01:25:14,900
While they have saved many lives, they expand with such force

1160
01:25:14,901 --> 01:25:17,960
that they have also caused some injuries and even deaths.

1161
01:25:19,240 --> 01:25:23,460
What we're trying to do is develop a sensing system in the seating positions

1162
01:25:23,461 --> 01:25:30,900
that identify if the seat is occupied by too small of a person, typically a child,

1163
01:25:31,600 --> 01:25:33,540
then we try and suppress the airbag.

1164
01:25:34,180 --> 01:25:38,580
We've also developed dual-stage or variable-stage inflators

1165
01:25:38,581 --> 01:25:43,840
that will actually respond to either the size or the location of the occupant.

1166
01:25:46,560 --> 01:25:49,700
Smarter airbags, along with automatic braking systems

1167
01:25:49,701 --> 01:25:52,280
which maintain the safe distance between cars,

1168
01:25:52,281 --> 01:25:55,780
will undoubtedly make future driving much safer.

1169
01:26:00,500 --> 01:26:05,340
Computers are the key to most of the smarter, safer, faster systems on today's cars.

1170
01:26:06,100 --> 01:26:11,420
Coming up, we'll see when these chips first work their way into the body shop.

1171
01:26:11,421 --> 01:26:25,421
The first patent for a windshield wiper was issued in 1903 to a woman named Mary Anderson.

1172
01:26:26,100 --> 01:26:34,900
The Body Shop will continue on Modern Marvels.

1173
01:26:34,901 --> 01:26:40,640
We now return to The Body Shop on Modern Marvels.

1174
01:26:40,641 --> 01:26:43,100
We've already dissected this car's body.

1175
01:26:43,101 --> 01:26:46,020
Now we're going to dissect its brain.

1176
01:26:46,021 --> 01:26:47,020
Thanks Chris.

1177
01:26:47,021 --> 01:26:50,480
Well, actually, all of its different brains.

1178
01:26:50,481 --> 01:26:54,960
Small computer chips like these appear in several locations throughout the car and do

1179
01:26:54,961 --> 01:26:59,740
a much better job than the big dumb mechanical systems of the past.

1180
01:26:59,741 --> 01:27:08,200
So when did the automotive world's mad scientist first try mating cars and computers?

1181
01:27:08,201 --> 01:27:13,500
Engineers in the 1950s and 60s were far too bulky to be installed in a car.

1182
01:27:13,501 --> 01:27:21,380
By 1970, engineers were right on the verge of combining these two technological wonders.

1183
01:27:21,381 --> 01:27:26,280
Back in those days, we filled a trunk full of computers and the supporting equipment

1184
01:27:26,281 --> 01:27:27,960
to go with them.

1185
01:27:27,961 --> 01:27:30,940
People thought, you'll never put a computer on a car.

1186
01:27:30,941 --> 01:27:34,180
And the reason was, was their mental model.

1187
01:27:34,181 --> 01:27:41,460
The smallest computer they had seen tended to fill up maybe a quarter of a room.

1188
01:27:41,461 --> 01:27:47,200
Within a few years after the invention of the microprocessor in 1971, computerized systems

1189
01:27:47,201 --> 01:27:53,380
to help the engine perform better shrank to the size of a car's battery.

1190
01:27:53,381 --> 01:27:58,360
The first systems led to electronic fuel injection, which increased power and fuel economy while

1191
01:27:58,361 --> 01:28:01,760
lowering pollution.

1192
